## 1. Introduction
Since the chief source of exploited energy on planet earth has been fossil fuels, their combustion has resulted in excessive emission of carbon dioxide $(\mathsf{C O}_{2})$ , with atmospheric concentration over $400\ \mathrm{ppm}$ in the year 2019. [1–3] In turn, excessive emission of carbon dioxide $(\mathsf{C O}_{2})$ has caused several environmental problems such as global warming, climatic turbulences which comprise the devastation of the whole ecosystem, seawater acidification, upsurge of ocean level, deviation in precipitation pattern, migration of wildlife habitats and its unpropitious effect on humans. [2] To mitigate these serious issues, the search for sustainable, renewable, and energy-efficient methods of direct conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ into valuable chemicals has become a prime theme for scientists and governments around the globe. [4] Various bona fide approaches have been executed for the capture, storage, utilization, and conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to reduce its atmospheric concentration and enhance the current energy supply. [5–7] However, the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bond present in ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ whose bond enthalpy is $805~\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ provides extra stability, necessitating a notable amount of energy input for the bond cleavage. [1,8] In recent years, the technology of chemical fixation, thermocatalysis [9], hydrogenation, photocatalysis, electrocatalysis, photoelectrocatalysis, and biochemical conversion have been deployed for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ utilization into assorted valuable chemicals such as carbon monoxide, methane, ethane, methanol, ethanol, formate, among others (Fig. 1). In this context, the quest for desirable nanoporous materials led to the exploration of assorted materials such as ionic liquids (ILs), zeolites, porous carbon, organic polymers, covalent organic frameworks (COFs), metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), and their derivatives. [10].
Among these, MOFs, an emerging class of crystalline hybrid porous material assembled with organic ligands and metal nodes/clusters, are preferred for various reasons. [11] MOFs have been extensively deployed to catalyze ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion reactions due to their high surface area, good stability and recyclability, permanent porosity, and multiple active centers. The timeline of progress for the catalytic applications of MOFs and their derivatives is illustrated in Fig. 2. The following are the unique set of properties, attributes, and reasoning for the applications of MOFs as catalysts: (i) By analyzing the molecular catalysts design, the structural flexibility and functional motility of the MOF components thrive to build MOF-based catalysts by embedding multiple catalytic sites, (ii) The regulated porosity of MOFs enables substrates and products to freely diffuse via MOF channels by assuring approachable active sites and efficient mass transport, (iii) The exceptionally large surface area, as well as designable framework topology of MOFs, provide the appropriate site to examine the mechanism of reaction processes at the molecular level, and (iv) Due to the high crystallinity, ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ can be adsorbed in higher concentrations at the active sites. [3,12–13].
As MOFs are heterogeneous catalysts, their confinement effect of the channel size inhibits the adsorbate-adsorbent interaction which fails to produce high ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ selectivity. The catalytic performance has been relatively poor for cyclic carbonate production, as high working pressures $(>3\ \mathsf{M P a})$ and temperatures $(>100~^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ are required, raising the cost due to the energy and capital input. [14–15] The long-term application of MOF materials is restricted, as the presence of comparatively weak metal–organic ligand coordination bonds renders MOFs vulnerable to degradation when subjected to diverse catalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction reactions. Moreover, there is the limited electrical conductivity of original MOFs, which significantly restricts their direct application in catalytic reduction reactions. [11] However, these pristine MOFs have been utilized as potential precursor/substrates to incorporate quantum dots (QDs), semiconductors, metal complexes, and metal nanoparticles (MNPs) inside the pores through post-synthetic modification (PSM) techniques to form MOFs composites, thus providing favorable circumstances for the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption in MOFs. [15] These MOF derivatives/composites, inheriting pore character from MOFs and the superb durability of the newly added constituents, have indeed been explored for catalytically reducing ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . This review focuses on the distinctive characteristics of MOFs-based nanocatalysts for directly converting $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ into valuable chemical products. Furthermore, the advantages and application of MOFs-based nanocatalysts are elaborated for catalytic $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ conversion via different methods such as photocatalysis, electrocatalysis, photoelectrocatalysis, and hydrogenation. Besides, the reaction of terminal alkynes with ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and cycloaddition reaction with epoxides to generate various valuable chemicals are discussed. Finally, the prospects for MOFs-based nanocatalysts have been expounded, highlighting their future outlooks in this research field. This comprehensive overview of the subject focusing on the various MOFs-based nanocatalysts reported since 2015 suggests that these nanocomposites have outstanding competence in the conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , making them intriguing candidates for real-world applications.
Although many reviews related to MOFs and their catalytic properties have been published in recent years, [3,16–28] our review offers a novel approach and stands apart in the contemporary literature. Most of the recent reviews present a brief and concise description and overview of recent advances in methods for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture and conversion. Beyond this, our own work presents a detailed comprehensive description of the general characteristics of MOFs and MOF-based nanocatalysts as well as the advantages of these nanocatalysts for the varied industrial application while critically analyzing their present shortcomings. Moreover, in-depth focus has been accorded to the various approaches for the sustainable catalytic conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ employing MOF-based nanocatalysts with inclusion of all primary research papers published since 2015. An inadequate thorough description of MOF’s interaction with functional material has been pervasive while we have strived to elaborate and highlight the relationship between ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture capacity of emerging MOF-based catalysts and their catalytic activity. All the prime advantageous features of MOFs facilitating the capture and conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ with appropriate examples are deliberated. Moreover, our review encompasses elaborate discussion on several additional pathways for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ utilization using MOFs-based nanocatalysts which have not been dealt with in other reviews, namely, carboxylation of terminal alkynes with ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ cycloaddition of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ with epoxides, reductive amination of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and hydrosilation reaction.
## 2. MOFs and MOFs-Based nanocatalysts
## 2.1. General characteristics
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), also characterized as porous coordination polymers (PCPs), are a relatively new class of crystalline porous organic–inorganic hybrid materials. The organic and inorganic components correspond to the organic linkers and the metal nodes, respectively (Fig. 3). The organic linkers of MOFs are immensely diverse and have a variety of configurations. The diverse assemblies of a vast number of metal clusters and organic linkers have led to the documentation of>20,000 MOFs. [29] Due to their unique properties, such as the ultra-high surface area, porosity, and crystallinity, MOFs have been widely used in various advanced applications such as gas storage, energy storage, drug delivery, gas sensing, heterogeneous catalysis, etc. [30–35].
## 2.2. Synthetic strategies for MOFs-based nanocatalysts
All the MOF-based catalysts can be categorized into three main types: (i) pristine MOFs, (ii) MOF composites, and (iii) MOF derivatives.
Pristine MOFs are metal-centered catalytic templates possessing highly organized morphology with consistent pore space and functional organic linkers. The metal nodes of the MOF structure have open coordination sites, which aid in the catalytic process without requiring significant structural alteration; also, the coordinatively unsaturated metal sites function well as Lewis acid catalysts. [20] A multifunctional MOF-based composite can be prepared via utilizing the PSM (post-synthetic modification) strategy and they are composed of one MOF along with various active components which usually include metal nanoparticles. The asconstructed composites exhibit unique attributes that outperform the individual components as they incorporate the characteristics of distinct MOF along with the properties of the additional active species. Recently, a fresh approach to constructing MOF-based composites via utilizing the pyrolysis procedure under critical conditions has piqued great interest wherein MOFs are used as precursors owing to their advantageous structural properties. [37] In this section, the general methods are briefly discussed for the synthesis of these three types of catalysts.
## 2.2.1. General methods for the synthesis of pristine MOFs
The various synthetic strategies for the assembly of pristine MOFs are summarized in Fig. 4 and discussed here. The various advantages and disadvantages of these methods have been summarized in Table 1.
(i) Solvothermal/Hydrothermal synthesis: It is the most commonly employed conventional synthesis method. It is a selfassembly approach wherein the MOF crystals grow from solutions containing sources for the metal node and organic linker. The reaction is carried out at high temperatures above the boiling point of the solvent, and at high pressures, in closed vessels. [37] Chen et al. reported the preparation of a novel $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -MOF, NNU-28, for the photoreduction of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ under visible light to produce formate. [38] Initially, they synthesized the carboxylate ligand, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}$ by Sonogashira coupling reaction. Subsequently, the solvothermal reaction between $\mathrm{\Delta}Z\mathrm{r}{\mathrm{Cl}_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}$ in the presence of DMF solvent and benzoic acid as a modulator generated the orange cubic crystals of the catalyst, NNU-28.
(ii) Microwave-assisted synthesis: This method makes use of microwave (MW) irradiation in an energy-efficient manner. The synthesis is influenced by the interaction of the microwaves with the reaction mixture, and therefore the selection of the solvent is of paramount importance [39] as MW-assisted heating offers much higher heating rates and facilitates even heating throughout the solution; MW-assisted process is a rapid and facile approach relative to the conventional hydro/solvothermal synthesis. Wang and co-workers developed a $[\mathrm{Cu}_{4}(\upmu_{3}-(\mathrm{OH})_{2}]$ -cluster-based MOF (CuMOF) via the MW-assisted protocol and employed it as a catalyst for the carboxylation of terminal alkynes with ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ under mild conditions. The sealed container encompassing the reaction mixture was irradiated in a MW oven at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $15\mathrm{min}$ . The ensuing reaction product (Cu-MOF) was centrifuged, washed, and then dried in a vacuum set-up. [40].
(iii) Mechanochemical synthesis: It is an environment-friendly approach that involves room temperature reactions under solvent-free conditions. The basic principle of mechanochemical synthesis is the mechanical cleavage of intramolecular bonds, which enforces a chemical transformation thus providing better yields in shorter reaction times. Chen et al. described the first synthesis of copper-based MOF-505 via an efficient liquid-assisted mechanochemical method where the type and amount of the assisted solvent had a considerably higher impact on the porosity and crystallinity of the reaction product than that of griding time.
Moreover, it was concluded that to facilitate the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture applications of MOF-5, it is imperative to carry out its mechanochemical synthesis on a large scale. [41].
(iv) Electrochemical synthesis: It is a promising and ecofriendly option for the synthesis of MOFs due to the deployment of ambient reaction conditions (low energy consumption), ease of scale-up, and a short crystal growth time. [39,42] In this process, instead of employing metal salts, metal ions are continuously delivered via anodic solution to the reaction medium containing dissolved linker molecules and a conducting salt. [43] This method is advantageous for industrial processes because of its ability to achieve greater solids content as compared to the normal batch reactions.39 Pirzadeh et al. successfully fabricated a $\mathsf{C u3}(\mathsf{B T C})2$ MOF (BTC $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylate) through an electrochemical route and employed it for CO2 and CH4 absorption; high yields $(\sim80\%)$ of $\mathsf{C u3}(\mathsf{B T C})2$ were obtained. [42].
(v) Sonochemical synthesis: Sonochemistry refers to the chemistry that occurs when high-energy ultrasound is applied to a reaction mixture with the basic premise to develop a simple, rapid, energy-efficient, and environmentally-friendly process that could be conducted at room temperature. [39] Jang and co-workers prepared a zeolite-like MOF with a rho topology through a sonochemical approach (rho-ZMOF(S)) [44] and compared its properties with the one synthesized by the conventional solvothermal route (rhoZMOF(C)). Uniform particles of high-quality rho-ZMOF(S) were produced only after $^\textrm{\scriptsize2h}$ while rho-ZMOF(C) was obtained after $72\ \mathrm{~h~}$ . Moreover, particles obtained from the sonochemical approach $\left\langle600\ \mathrm{nm}\right\rangle$ were found to be significantly smaller in size as compared to the ones obtained via the solvothermal approach $(60~\upmu\mathrm{m})$ .
## 2.2.2. General methods for the synthesis of MOFs-composites
(i) Ship-in-bottle strategy: It is also called ’in-situ generation in MOFs’ wherein the embedded material precursors are inserted into the cavities of initially synthesized MOFs via diffusion or other methods, accompanied by in situ transformations of desired materials in the MOF cavities using chemical reduction, photoreduction, thermolysis, or other methods (Fig. 5 a). The drawback of this synthetic approach is the disruption of the internal structures of MOF templates during the formation process of embedded materials [46] with a lack of control of the morphology. In order to effectively capture ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , Yoo et al. introduced polyaniline (PANI) into a highly porous MOF, MIL-101(Cr) via the ship-in-bottle method. Upon loading of PANI, although the porosity of MOF was reduced, there was a significant increase in the absorption capacity and selectivity for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . [47].
(ii) Bottle-around-ship strategy: The build-bottle-around-ship, also known as ‘surface-modified encapsulation’ involves two basic steps: (i) NPs with consistent size, structure, and morphology are produced, (ii) MOFs shells are then grown on NPs that have already been synthesized (Fig. 5 b). [46,48] Herein, bridges are made up of structure-flexible materials that can be utilized to link MOFs with encapsulated compounds. This helps to reduce the prominent lattice structure barriers that exist between MOFs and encapsulated materials, causing MOFs to self-nucleate rather than create the encapsulation structure. Unfortunately, the structureperformance relationship of these composites is difficult to examine because of the complex interfacial structures. Marandi and coworkers employed a bottle-around-ship strategy to synthesize CoPOM@MIL-101(Cr) composite. [49] The as-prepared composite served as an efficient catalytic system for the solvent-free cycloaddition of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to epoxides under ambient conditions.
(iii) One-pot synthesis: It is the simplest and the most efficient method, wherein MOF composites are prepared in a single step (Fig. 5 c). The metal NPs are first prepared using a suitable solvent and then the core–shell structure is formed spontaneously with the help of precursors. Zhao and their team fabricated a photocatalyst for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , based on $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) and reduced graphene oxide (rGO) via a facile ‘‘one-pot” method. [50] The rGO and $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) composite were prepared using a solvothermal process. The $\operatorname{rGO}(\omega$ $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125composite displayed a twofold enhanced yield $(1,116\ \upmu\mathrm{molg}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1};$ as compared to pure MIL-125.
## 2.2.3. General methods for the synthesis of MOF-derivatives
(i) Thermal treatment: MOFs-based nanostructures can be prepared by utilizing MOFs as precursors or sacrificial templates. In this regard, thermal treatment of the precursors is the most commonly employed method. There are two different strategies for thermal treatment- pyrolysis, and calcination in the presence of air. [51] By adjusting the reaction parameters such as temperature, heating rate, and reaction time, various derivatives with distinct morphology, porosity, composition, and structures can be produced. [52].
(ii) Ion-exchange method: It is a newly-explored alternative strategy. Hu et al. employed HKUST-1 as a precursor for fabricating a three-component composite catalyst to efficiently perform ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ photoreduction. [53] Carbon-coated metal sulfides $(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C u}_{2\mathrm{-}\mathrm{x}}S)$ were synthesized by mixing HKUST-1 and thioacetamide (TAA) under stirring followed by calcination. It was then loaded on $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ leading to the formation of a three-component heterojunction ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{-C u}}_{2}$ - $\mathrm{_{x}}S@\mathrm{g}_{-}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ (Fig. 6). The as-prepared composite showed $\sim7-$ and 23-times enhanced reactivity as compared to bare $\mathsf{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ and C$\mathsf{C u}_{2-\mathbf{x}}S.$ , respectively.
## 2.3. Applications of MOFs-based nanocatalysts in $C O_{2}$ conversion
The application of MOFs in heterogeneous catalysis is one of the most exploited fields as they are excellent catalysts owing to their highly ordered porous structure, functional organic linkers, ultrahigh surface area, and metal centers. The catalytically active metal sites can be introduced in the MOFs by metalation of ligands present on the MOF linkers either during MOF synthesis or by postsynthesis modifications. Also, the MOFs can be coupled with other nanostructures to improve their conductivity and to produce hybrid nanocomposites for more efficient and specific catalysis. The uniform, as well as tunable pore shapes and sizes of MOFs, make them suitable candidates for shape-selective catalysis. [20] The functional synergy between the MOFs and the guest nanoparticles (NPs) located inside the MOF cavity offers multiple integrated advantages thereby offsetting their shortcomings; enhanced performance in heterogeneous catalysis is obtained which is not attainable by the individual components. [48,54]
The combination of MOFs and MNPs, particularly for the application in catalysis, has garnered immense interest from diverse researchers in recent years. MOFs are considered to be the robust platform for encapsulation of guest nanoparticles due to various reasons: [54–56] (i) MOFs possess high surface areas and permanent porosity, (ii) The highly ordered and well-defined pore structures of MOFs provide a clear surrounding environment for the NPs, (iii) The aggregation and leaching issues generally taking place during catalysis are minimized due to the confinement and electronic effects offered by MOFs, (iv) MOFs can accommodate various guest species as they are highly adaptable to catalytic design.
With an outstanding unique set of properties, the MOFs materials offer fascinating catalytic applications; one among many is the catalysis of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ capture and conversion reactions, for which the noteworthy adsorption capacity of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ by MOFs forms the basis. In this regard, the functionalization of MOFs with various types of active sites offers improved catalytic activity. Moreover, MOFs can form hybrid materials and can function as templates to form MOF composites or derivatives with hierarchical porosities and precise morphologies to achieve desired prowess in catalysis. [56] Earlier, the utilization of MOFs for electrochemical ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion had been restricted because of the low charge carrier mobility, but recent studies uncover many novel opportunities to enhance the conductivity of MOFs material. The advancement of electrocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion using MOFs is primarily due to the highly ordered porous nature of MOFs system that facilitates free movement of ions and soluble ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . [57] Various studies for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ hydrogenation reactions reveal that MOF-based materials perform as efficient catalysts for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion as well as offer additional benefits including modifiable structures, porosity, high stability, etc. [58] Further, in certain conditions, the MOFs are thermally decomposed to obtain the consistent distribution of active sites for enhanced chemical as well as thermal stability. [56]
MOFs-based catalysts also exhibit remarkable performance in catalyzing the reactions of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ with epoxides and terminal alkynes to produce cyclic carbonates and alkynyl carboxylic acids, respectively, which are commercially important entities in synthetic and pharmaceutical industries.
## 2.4. Advantages of MOFs-based nanocatalysts in $C O_{2}$ conversion
The practical applications of conventional catalysts like zeolites, silica, and other porous materials have been limited due to factors like poor recyclability, short lifetime, and low efficiency. MOFs possess remarkable advantages over traditional catalysts in terms of recyclability and the ease of catalyst removal. MOFs can perform efficient catalysis even at low ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ concentrations as they can capture a high concentration of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ around the catalytically active sites through ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption. [59].
The main attributes of an outstanding catalyst for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction are high selectivity, activity, and efficiency. Selectivity depends on chemical properties (such as oxidizing and reducing power, and binding affinity), the activity of the catalyst is governed by the structural features, and the efficiency is determined by the morphological and optoelectronic properties. [60] MOFs, which are based on reticular chemistry, offer an ideal platform that supports all the three major attributes of an excellent catalyst. They facilitate easy incorporation of molecular catalysts into the backbone of their frameworks which regulates the selectivity. The high activity is rendered by the easy diffusion of substrates to the active sites owing to the large surface area and tunable porosity. [61–62] MOFs composed of redox-active metals and open metal sites (or open coordination sites) are found to be highly effective $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction catalysts. [60,63] Functionalization and conjugation of the organic linker in the MOF fosters bandgap engineering, particularly useful in photoreduction and photoelectroreduction. Yet another design principle for a remarkable MOF-based photocatalyst is the integration of plasmonic nanoparticles which intensify the incident light locally. In the case of electrocatalysis, by regulating the thickness of the MOF films, an optimal balance in the interplay between mass and electron transport can be achieved, which augments the efficiency of the catalyst.
Some of the significant advantages presented by MOFs for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction are: (1) The unique porosity of MOFs allows the confinement of various catalytically active species to enhance their catalytic activity; (2) MOFs show efficient recyclability due to their high stability; (3) catalytically active sites of MOFs are uniformly distributed and are easily accessible for catalysis. [1,64] In the case of photocatalytic reduction of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , MOFs facilitate improved electron-hole separation, and also, the multifunctional organic ligands present in the MOFs play the role of ‘‘light capture antenna” by effectively accepting the protons. [1] Some of the prime advantages of MOF-based catalysts are depicted in Fig. 7. The low charge carrier mobility of MOFs limits their application in electroreduction processes. However, some novel strategies have been developed to induce conductivity in MOFs which make them potential electrocatalysts for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction reactions. MOFs have been widely used as electrocatalysts as they allow the free movement of ions and dissolved ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ in the matrix, owing to the porous and heterogeneous network created by them. [57].
## 3. The relationship between $\mathbf{CO_{2}}$ capture capacity and catalytic activity
Efficient ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption and activation are of prime importance in the catalytic conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . Owing to their large specific surface area, uniform porosity, and chemical tunability, MOFs have emerged as very promising materials with high ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption capacity, suitable for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction reactions even at low ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ concentrations. [65–67] As opposed to homogeneous catalysts, the high ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture capacity of MOF-based nanocatalysts leads to an increase in the local concentration of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ around the catalytically active sites. Thus, the MOF pores function as ‘‘microreactors”, conducive to reactions between ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and substrates, boosting the reaction yields and selectivities. [1,66] Moreover, the presence of particular functional groups in MOFs also improves the catalytic efficiency by their ability to augment the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ uptake and activate ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ molecules and/or other reactants. [66] Furthermore, the fact that the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ uptake and selectivities of MOF-based materials facilitate high catalytic efficiency even in a mixed-gas environment with low concentrations or partial pressures of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , is of great significance. [66,68–71].
Among the reported MOFs, CPO-27-Mg is found to display the highest ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ uptake (about $35.2~\mathrm{wt\%}$ . Besides this, the presence of a large number of open alkaline metal sites $(\mathrm{Mg}^{2+})$ in its structure is believed to be highly beneficial for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ activation. [72–73] In the photoreduction process, ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption is the rate-limiting step due to the fact that the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ molecule is very stable and chemically inert, with linear geometry. [74–75] In this context, the superior photocatalytic activity of $_{\mathrm{CPO-}27-M g/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}}$ in the conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to methane $\mathrm{(CH_{4})}$ can be attributed to the aforementioned qualities of the CPO-Mg-27 MOF. [76] Experimental results have revealed that the incorporation of CPO-27- Mg led to a significant increase in the amount of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ uptake from the original value of $19.94~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ for $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ to $106.59\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ for the composite.
Ligand modification and functionalization are now proven strategic routes to improve the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ uptake of MOFs. The incorporation of polar groups like –OH, $\boldsymbol{-}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{N}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}_{2}$ , and –COOH into the organic ligands very effectively enhances the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption due to increased ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ -ligand affinity, resulting from stronger ligand interactions of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ molecules. [77–78] Accordingly, the aminofunctionalized $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -Uio-66, prepared by Sun et al., displayed higher ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption capability $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ uptake reached as high as $68~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ than its parent Uio-66(Zr) $(53\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ . [79] Further, the partial substitution of the organic linker ATA (2- aminoterephthalic acid) by DTA (2,5-diaminoterephthalate) in $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -Uio-66 revealed better photocatalytic performance in ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion due to boosted ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption and optical absorption. Similarly, the multi-amino functionalized Zirconium MOF, ZrSDCA- ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , displayed improved ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption capability, with the amount of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ uptake reaching $83.8~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ and $35.5~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ at $273\mathrm{~K~}$ and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ , respectively.
Kajiwara and coworkers triumphantly demonstrated the synergistic effect between the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption properties and catalytic activity of MOF composite catalysts. [80] They employed a facile post-synthetic exchange method to incorporate a photoactive $\mathsf{R}\mathsf{u}^{\mathrm{II}}$ - CO complex into UiO-67 MOF. Experimental results revealed that the turnover number for product formation is higher for $\mathsf{R u}^{\mathrm{{II}}}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C O}$ complex catalyst than that of the UiO-67/RuCO composite but the catalytic activity is substantially reduced with a decrease in partial pressure of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . This indicates that the catalytic activity of the molecular catalyst is greatly dependent on the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ concentration in the reaction mixture. Whereas, UiO-67/RuCO composite catalyst retained its catalytic performance even under a $5\ \%\ C O_{2}$ atmosphere, facilitated by the synergy between the adsorptive sites and catalytically active sites of the MOF, capable of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ enrichment in the pores.
A Ni metal–organic framework monolayer (Ni MOLs) accomplished the photocatalytic reduction of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to carbon monoxide (CO) in simulated flue gas $(10\%\mathsf{C O}_{2})$ with an outstanding CO selectivity of $96.8\%$ . The theoretical and experimental results attributed the combined effect of stronger ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption energy $(-200.11~\mathrm{kJ}/\$ mol) and the weaker $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorption energy $\left(-64.78~\mathrm{kJ/mol}\right)$ being responsible for the upgraded activity and selectivity to generate CO with the suppression of $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ by-product. This example is of great significance for the practical implementation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ photoreduction since in reality, the concentration of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ is low in the exhaust from heavy industries and factories, and research on the scope of efficient photocatalytic conversion of diluted ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ is still in its nascent stage.
## 4. Catalytic transformations of $\mathbf{CO_{2}}$ using MOFs-based nanocatalysts via different methods
Recent years have witnessed tremendous and impressive innovations in employing nanocatalysts derived from and/or based on MOFs for the conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to various value-added chemicals/- fuels. In this section, we elaborate on the fundamentals and principles of various catalytic conversions of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , with an in-depth discussion on the recently developed MOFs-based nanocatalysts. The various methods for catalytic conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ are listed in Fig. 8. Moreover, the advantages and disadvantages of these methods are summarized in Fig. 9.
## 4.1. Photoreduction of $C O_{2}$
The photocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction is a proton-assisted multielectron reduction process, driven by either visible light or UV radiations. However, the one-electron reduction of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ is thermodynamically unfavorable due to the highly negative reduction potential of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ $\slash{\mathsf{C O}}_{2}^{-}$ $_{-1.90\vee}$ vs NHE, at $\mathsf{p H}7.00\}$ while the comparatively lower reduction potential (vs NHE at $\mathsf{p H}7.00\rangle$ , as shown in equations 1–5, renders the proton-assisted multielectron ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction processes much more favorable. [82] The conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ into CO is a two-electron process, possessing a relatively low kinetic barrier as compared to some other conversion processes like the reduction of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to yield $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , which are accomplished by the transfer of six and eight electrons respectively, signifying the increased kinetic energy barrier. [1].
$\mathrm{CO}_{2}+2\mathrm{H}^{+}+2\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow\mathrm{HCOOH}$ $\mathsf{E}_{1}=-0.61$ V (1).   
$\mathrm{CO}_{2}+2\mathrm{H}^{+}+2\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow\mathrm{CO}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\mid$ $\mathsf{E}_{2}=-0.53\mathrm{~V~}$ (2).   
$\mathrm{CO}_{2}+4\mathrm{H}^{+}+4\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow\mathrm{HCHO}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\mathrm{~E}_{3}=-0.48^{\circ}$ V (3).   
$\mathrm{CO}_{2}+6\mathrm{H}^{+}+6\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}~\mathrm{E}_{4}=-0.38$ (4).   
$\mathrm{CO}_{2}+8\mathrm{H}^{+}+8\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow\mathrm{CH}_{4}+2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\mathrm{E}_{5}=-0.24\mathrm{V}$ (5).   
