## 4.1.1. MOFs-based photocatalysts
Metal-organic frameworks comprise metal ions or clusters and organic linkers. By functionalization of metal nodes and modification of the organic linkers (via the introduction of aminofunctionalized linkers, metal ligands, photosensitizer-modified linkers, electron-rich conjugated linkers, etc.), the MOF can be tailored such that it possesses enhanced light-harvesting ability and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ uptake capacity, favorable conditions for high photocatalytic efficiency. [64,84] The metal clusters function as inorganic semiconductor quantum dots (QDs) and are the reduction sites of the adsorbed ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . The organic linkers may be regarded as antennae to absorb light and activate the metal clusters. [56,85–88] The exceptional porosity of the MOFs ensures the suppression of recombination of photogenerated electron-hole pairs, by facilitating proximate contact of the reactant to the catalytic active sites. Consequently, the photoinduced electrons can effectively migrate from the photoexcited organic ligand to the neighboring catalytic centers. [64].
Pristine MOFs: Xu et al. successfully presented the relationship between photoinduced charge carrier separation efficiency and photocatalytic activity [89] while conducting the visible-lightdriven $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction deploying a photoactive porphyrin-based MOF, PCN-222. The transient absorption (TA) spectroscopy and photoluminescence (PL) spectroscopy observations unveiled that the effective suppression of electron-hole recombination was due to the emergence of a huge electron trap state in PCN-222. Consequently, the supply of long-lifetime electrons highly boosted the photoreduction efficiency. Sadeghi and co-workers prepared another porphyrin-based MOF, Zn/PMOF in which TCPP (Tris (1- chloro-2-propyl) phosphate) was employed as a ligand. The reaction was carried out under UV/Visible light using $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ vapor as an electron donor. During the $^\mathrm{~4~h~}$ photocatalytic reaction, $10.43~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}$ of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ was formed, which is $80.6~\%$ higher than the unadorned ZnO. [90]
The light absorption capability of the catalyst plays a key role in its photocatalytic efficiency. Chen and co-workers prepared a novel $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -MOF, NNU-28, using a carboxylate ligand-responsive to visible light, developed from an anthracene group; [38] the stable photocatalyst exhibited broad visible light absorption up to $650~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . It possessed a remarkable photoreduction activity with a formate formation rate of $183.3\upmu\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ mmolM1 . Electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) studies and the photocatalytic experiments demonstrated that both, the anthracene-based organic ligand and the zirconium oxo cluster, served as the photocatalytic centers. Since the increase in molecular conjugation degree substantially improves the light absorption, [91] the scope of combining conjugated dienes and amino groups to MOFs has been studied. To this end, another light-responsive Zr-MOF was prepared by Sun et al., deploying conjugated amine-functionalized dicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{\cdotH_{2}L}$ , ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SDCA-NH}}_{2}^{\prime}$ as the organic ligand [92] which displayed extensive visible light absorption with the band edge at $\sim600\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Photoluminescence, Mot-Schotky measurements, and the photocatalytic experiment revealed the presence of the dual catalytic routes- through the photoactive $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S D C A}$ ligand and the $Z\mathrm{r}_{6}$ oxo cluster was responsible for the enhanced photocatalytic activity with formate formation rate of $96.2~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ mmolM1 .
Wang, Liu, and co-workers investigated the role of the morphology of MOF in photocatalysis and offered insights for further developments of excellent MOF-materials for photocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction, by controlling their morphology. [93] They synthesized a series of ZIF-67 nanocrystals for use as cocatalyst in the photoconversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to CO, in the presence of a ruthenium-based molecular complex as the photosensitizer (Fig. 11). The experimental results revealed that the two-dimensional ZIF-67 with leaf-like morphology showed the highest photocatalytic performance. The superior photoactivity is ascribed to its supreme ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ uptake capacity and systematized transfer of electrons at the interface between $\mathsf{[R u(b p y)]^{2+}}$ and the MOF. In contrast, the ZIF-67 nanocrystals with rhombic dodecahedral morphology showed lower ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption ability and photocatalytic activity. Recently, two Ni-MOF catalysts of different forms were synthesized by Song and coworkers, to compare their catalytic performance. [94] Ni$\mathsf{M O F}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})$ offered low charge transfer resistance, lower recombination of charge carriers, higher yield of CO $34\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ vs $18\ \mathrm{\textmumol}$ ), and displayed a longer lifetime (5 h vs 2 h) as compared to Ni-MOF.
MOF-composites: While the bridging ligands of the MOFs or the guest species immobilized in the MOF matrix serve as lightharvesting units, the light-absorption is found to be reduced when the light-harvesting units are buried deep inside MOF layers. This issue can be surmounted by the incorporation of metal nanoparticles in the MOF which offer a higher optical absorption crosssection. Furthermore, the mass transport of substrates and products is often hampered in the micropores of bulky MOF matrices as opposed to the MOF surface. In this context, Chen et al. illustrated that thin nanosheets of porphyrin paddle-wheel frameworks-3 (PPF-3) facilitated better mass transport and charge separation than thick nanosheets. [95] Moreover, plasmonic gold nanoparticles loaded on the surface of MOF offered significantly higher optical absorption than gold nanoparticles (AuNPs) incorporated into MOF matrices.
The MOF, ZIF-8, displays high surface area, presence of even pores and channels on the surface, excellent stability, and significant ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption ability in liquid medium. [96–97] However, due to its high band gap energy $(4.9\:\mathrm{eV})$ , it shows absorption only in the UV region. [98] There is more research potential for the development of ZIF-8 photocatalysts for visible-light-driven photoconversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . In this context, the integration of Cu metal over ZIF-8 lowers the bandgap energy and realizes the absorption of visible light. Goyal et al. made a pioneering attempt at methanol production from ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ using Cu/ZIF-8 catalysts, under visible light irradiation, as depicted in Fig. 12a. [99] They analyzed the effect of solvent concentration and Cu loading on the morphology of the ensued catalysts; $2\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8\ \mathrm{N}2$ , prepared in $2\mathrm{~M~}$ ammonium hydroxide, by treating ZIF-8 with 2 mmol copper, manifested the highest photocatalytic activity, as evident from Fig. 12b. It was found that the increase in molar concentration of the solvent decreased the crystal size from $200\ \mathrm{nm}$ to $50\mathrm{nm}$ , and transformed the cubic crystal structure to orthorhombic. Owing to its good stability in water and excellent adsorption capability for dissolved ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , Liu et al. grew ZIF-8 nanoparticles on $Z\mathrm{n}_{2}\mathrm{GeO}_{4}$ nanorods. The prepared $\mathrm{Zn_{2}G e O_{4}}/\mathrm{ZIF}{-8}$ nanocomposite exhibited $62\%$ enhanced photoreduction performance for $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ formation as compared to pure $Z_{\mathrm{{In_{2}G e O_{4}}}}$ nanorods [96] Fig. 13.
Various catalytically active species like metal nanoparticles, photoactive semiconductors, and quantum dots (QDs) have been extensively studied and have been substantiated to accomplish efficient charge separation and enhanced light absorption for photocatalysis. [1,100] Owing to their permanent porosity and large surface area, the MOFs can act as host matrices for encapsulating the guest species into the tuneable cavities or, the guest species may be enclosed by MOF layers forming a core–shell structure. Such MOF composites besides inheriting the advantages of both the MOF and guest species, possess better and improved features such as (1) enhanced stability and mechanical properties, (2) availability of long-lived photogenerated electrons, and accelerated electron-hole separation, due to the generation of electrontrapping sites and heterojunction, and (3) improved $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption capacity than the parent MOF, as a result of new interactions at the interphase, generation of new pore environment(s) and more affinity of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to the guest species. [1,66].
The integration of MNPs into MOF is a highly efficient strategy to enhance photoactivity; they can either be incorporated inside the pores of MOFs $(\mathsf{M N P s@M O F})$ or can be loaded on the external surface of MOF (MNPs/MOF). The MOF acting as elegant host matrices can help prevent the agglomeration of the photoactive MNPs. The separation and transfer of photoinduced electron-hole pairs can be boosted, as the MNPs can function as electrontrapping sites (or electron-acceptors) and restrain electron-hole recombination. [54,101–104] Additionally, some noble metal NPs such as copper, silver, and gold NPs exhibit localized surface plasmon resonance (LSPR) effect, which significantly enhances the visible-light harvesting ability of the composites. [3] Chen et al. demonstrated the effect of doping ultrasmall Ag nanoparticles on MOFs for boosting their photoactivity and selectivity. [105] Photoreduction of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to CO was realized using $\mathsf{A g@C o}$ -ZIF-9 photocatalyst, with the help of a photosensitizer. The experimental results revealed that the increase in the loading amount of Ag NPs enhanced the CO formation, effectively suppressing the competing $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ evolution. The photoactivity of Ag@Co-ZIF-9 was twice that of the bare Co-ZIF-9 and the selectivity improved about $20\%$ for 0.5-hour visible-light irradiation. To examine the size effect of MOF in the photocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction, Guo and co-workers prepared MIL-101(Cr)-Ag hybrids with controlled sizes of the MOF, spanning from 80 to $800\mathrm{nm}$ , as shown in Figure 13.106 The experimental results unveiled that the sample with the smallest MOF size $(80\ \mathrm{nm})$ showed the best photoactivity with product formation rates of $808.2~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ for CO and $427.5\ \mathrm{\textmumol\g}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . The higher photocatalytic activity can be ascribed to the higher density of unit cells on the corners and edges of the hybrid, which facilitates improved electron transfer capability. In another study, researchers immobilized Pt NPs inside, outside, or inside and outside the zirconium MOF, $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -UiO-68. [102] Effectual charge transfer from $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -UiO-68 was achieved in the photocatalytic process by the formation of $\mathrm{\sfPt}$ -MOF heterojunctions. This prevented the recombination of photoinduced charge carriers increasing photoactivity.
Deng et al. successfully highlighted the structural advantages of hollow structural MOFs and $\mathsf{M N P s@M O F}$ composites over traditional pristine MOFs. [103] They solvothermally modified Co-ZIF67 to shape-defined hollow structural Co-MOF-74 and prepared AgNPs@Co-MOF-74 as well. Using the samples of conventionally prepared Co-MOF-74, hollow-structural Co-MOF-74, and AgNPS@MOF-74, as the cocatalysts in the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ -to-CO photoreduction, they illustrated the structural superiority of these catalysts. The experimental results unveiled that the amount of CO produced witnessed a rise of 1.8 times for hollow structural MOF and 3.8 times for $\mathsf{A g N P s@M O F}$ as compared to Co-MOF-74 as the cocatalyst. These results confirm that the greater external surface area of the hollow structure exposes a higher number of co-catalytic active sites and the thin shell renders confined space for easy diffusion of reactants, thereby boosting the co-catalytic efficiency. The highest catalytic activity of AgNPs@MOF can be ascribed to the LSPR effect of silver NPs.
Integrating semiconductors with matched band gap with a MOF is an authenticated strategy to achieve improved separation efficiency of photoinduced electrons. [3,100] The semiconductors that are most commonly used for developing MOF composites are $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , $Z\mathrm{n}_{2}\mathrm{GeO}_{4},$ CdS, and carbon nitride (CN). $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is regarded as the benchmark semiconductor for photocatalysis, owing to its nontoxic nature, good chemical and physical stability, and low cost. [107–109] However, its photocatalytic efficiency is far from being satisfactory as its optical absorption is limited to the UV spectrum due to its high bandgap $(3.0{-}3.2\ \mathrm{eV})$ [8] besides its low ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption ability. [109] Therefore, the formulation of more efficient, visible-light responsive $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ photocatalysts by assorted methods is essential for the effective utilization of solar energy for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion. Zou and co-workers synthesized a series of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ nanocomposites via the grinding and solid-synthesis methods; [67] nanocomposites prepared via the grinding method exhibited superior photocatalytic performance due to the availability of extra spaces in the structure for capturing ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . When the photoreaction was carried out via the solid–gas mode, without the use of sacrificial agents and photosensitizers, ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ was reduced to CO at a formation rate of $21.74~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ with $99\%$ selectivity, as shown in Fig. 14 (B). Whereas, the photocatalysts prepared via the solidsynthesis method, wherein $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ was encapsulated within the ZIF-8 framework, showed lower photocatalytic activity. It was concluded that the difference in structural features as a result of the different synthetic methods deployed determined the photocatalytic performance.
In a study, Khaletskaya et al. deposited gold nanoparticles (GNPs) onto the surface of amino-functionalized $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 and fabricated a $\mathsf{G N P/T i O}_{2}$ composite via the pyrolytic conversion of $\mathsf{G N P/N H}_{2}$ -MIL-125 nanocrystals. The ${\bf G N P/T i O}_{2}$ nanocomposite showed a considerably enhanced photocatalytic activity than the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ samples. [110] Crake and co-workers employed an in-situ growth method for the synthesis of ${\mathsf{M O F}}/{\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}$ bifunctional materials where the enhanced interactions between $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -UiO66 resulted in the formation of a heterojunction, which allowed effective charge separation. These nanocomposites almost doubled the photocatalytic reduction of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to CO, compared with pure $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . [108].
The construction of a Z-scheme heterojunction to boost the photocatalytic activity of semiconductors has proven to be very effective. An artificial Z-scheme photocatalyst mimicks the natural photosynthetic process and comprises an oxidation semiconductor (which has a low valence band position) and a reduction semiconductor (which has a high conduction band position) which are joined together. [111] Upon light irradiation, the electrons get excited from the valence bands (VB) to the conduction bands (CB) of both photocatalysts and thereby generate photoinduced holes in their VBs. Further, the photogenerated charge carriers follow a Z-shaped pathway wherein the photoexcited electrons in the CB of the oxidation semiconductor transfer to recombine with the photogenerated holes in the valence band of the reduction semiconductor (Fig. 15). Consequently, the photoinduced holes (possessing high oxidation power) in VB of oxidation semiconductor and electrons (with high reduction power) in CB of reduction semiconductor are preserved. [112] Thus, a Z-scheme photocatalyst utilizes light efficiently, preserves strong redox capability, and offers seamless charge separation. Meng et al. prepared $(0{-}Z{\bmod{/}}\Gamma\mathsf{G O}/\$ UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ Z-scheme heterojunction via a facile solvothermal method. The aforementioned heterostructure could effectively reduce ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ into methanol and formic acid under the illumination of visible light, in which the yields of methanol and formic acid were 34.85 and $6.40~{\upmu\mathrm{molg}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}}$ respectively. [113] Most of the MOF-semiconductor nanocomposites reported in the literature are prepared by wet-chemistry methods, which often entails aggravations involving many steps and being time-consuming. [114] Slow nucleation, poor crystal growth rate, uneven mixing, and plodding heat transfer are some of the other issues. He, Gan, and co-workers presented the first report on an aerosol route for the quick and scalable formation of HKUST-1 and HKUST-1 $/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanocomposite in micrometer-sized droplets, which successfully circumvented all the aforementioned limitations of wetchemistry methods. [115] The as-prepared photocatalysts exhibited high surface area, noteworthy photostability, and remarkable photoreduction efficiency for the conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to CO.
The integration of graphitic carbon nitride (CN) into MOF has been utilized in photocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion. [3,53,116] The limitation of quick recombination of electron-hole pairs in bulk CN, is overcome by employing 2D atomic carbon nitride nanosheets (CNNS). However, the low ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of CN remains a lingering concern. [3] Shi et al. reported the preparation of a heterogeneous photocatalyst, UiO-66/CNNS, via an electrostatic self-assembly method, for the conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to CO. [117] The composite exhibited a large surface area, facilitated the prolonged lifetime of photoinduced electron-hole pairs, significant stability, and reusability. The superior ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture ability can be attributed to the noteworthy adsorption capability of UiO-66 MOF. In another study, Liu and his team grafted the surface of holey $\mathsf{g}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ nanotubes with ZIF-8 nanoclusters. [118] The optical absorption and enhanced charge separation capabilities of tubular $\mathsf{g}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ combined with the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of ZIF-8 nanoclusters bestowed the nanocomposite with high photocatalytic performance for $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ formation. BIF-20 is a porous boron imidazolate framework that resembles zeolite and possesses a great density of exposed B-H bonding. Xu and co-workers employed a facile electrostatic self-assembly technique to generate BIF- $20@\mathrm{g}_{-}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ nanosheets by combining BIF-20 with $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ nanosheets. The ensued nanocomposite preferably absorbed ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and efficiently photoreduced it. As compared to $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ nanosheets, this composite showed 9.7 times and 9.85 times enhanced evolution rates for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and CO, respectively. [116].
${\sf R e}^{\mathrm{I}}({\sf C O})_{3}(\mathrm{BPYDC}){\sf C l}$ is used as a photocatalyst for the conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to CO. Its covalent attachment to UiO-67 $\mathbf{\partial}\cdot\mathbf{Re}_{\mathrm{n}}{-}\mathbf{MOF})$ leads to its deactivation due to the prevention of dimerization. In 2016, Choi et al. reported a work in which they systematically controlled the density of the photocatalyst over the framework. [119] The photocatalytic activity for ${\mathrm{Re}}_{3}–{\mathrm{MOF}}$ turned out to be the highest. They coated ${\mathrm{Re}}_{3}–{\mathrm{MOF}}$ onto Ag nanocubes ${\bf\ddot{A}B}\subset{\bf R e}_{3}–{\bf M O F}.$ , causing photoactive Re centers to be restricted around the amplified nearsurface electric fields at the surface of $\mathsf{A g}$ nanocubes which resulted in a sevenfold increase in the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to CO conversion under the visible light; up to $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ of long-term stability was also achieved. Ryu et al. found that a right balance in the vicinity of the Re complex ${\mathrm{Re}}^{\mathrm{I}}({\mathrm{CO}})_{3}({\mathrm{BPYDC}})({\mathrm{Cl}})$ $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BPYDC}=2,2^{\prime}$ -bipyridine- $.5{,}5^{\prime}.$ - dicarboxylic acid), and amine-functionalized multiple ligands in MOFs, represented as re-MOF- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ can be used to create a molecular photocatalyst with high activity and selectivity (Fig. 16). [120] The intermolecular stabilization of carbamate intermediates has shown to result in a 3-fold improvement in photoactivity for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to CO reduction.
In 2018, the unprecedented integration of inorganic semiconductors with molecular redox catalysts through MOF was reported for the photoreduction of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . [121] The ternary CdS/UiO-bpy-Co composite prepared by Chen et al., via the integration of CdS with molecular redox catalysts through UiO-bpy $(\mathsf{b p y}=2,2^{\prime}$ -bipyridine) MOF, showed an excellent photocatalytic activity with a yield of $235\upmu\mathrm{mol}\up g^{-1}\up h^{-1}$ CO, with the selectivity $0f{>}85\%$ . The CO evolution rate was 10.2 times enhanced in comparison to that of the parent CdS. He et al. designed a MOF-based ternary nanocomposite $\left(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\right.$ $\mathsf{C u}_{2}\mathsf{O}/\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{B T C})_{2})$ which exhibited high ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion efficiency. Also, the integration of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ on $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathsf{B T C})_{2}$ led to the preferential formation of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . The improved electron-hole pair density and effective ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ activation by the coordinatively unsaturated metal sites led to the enhanced performance of the catalyst. [122] Wang et al. demonstrated the preparation of ternary $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O@{Cu@UiO-66-}}$ $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ nanocubes possessing p-n junctions [123] wherein the solvothermal method was employed for the simultaneous production of $\mathtt{C u}$ from $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ and coating of the $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ nanoparticles with $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ . The advantages of high visible-light absorption by UiO-66- $\cdot\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ and $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ coupled with the excellent charge separation and migration of Cu metal resulted in an outstanding photocatalytic efficiency for the ternary heterostructure. Han et al. prepared a uniform and highly dispersed MOF-74 nanoshuttle by assembling the $\mathsf{A u@P d@M O F–74}$ , Pt/MOF-74, and $\mathsf{P t}/\mathsf{\Lambda}$ $\mathsf{A u@P d@M O F–74}$ nanocatalysts (Fig. 17). Au@Pd@MOF-74 photocatalyzed ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ with $100\%$ selectivity, whereas Pt/MOF-74 catalyzed ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion at $99.7\ \%$ CO selectivity in the reverse water gas shift reaction (RWGS). Interestingly, $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{MOF}-74$ not only exhibited remarkable CO selectivity $(100\%)$ in the RWGS but also photocatalyzed ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to methane at a low concentration. Hence, this work proposed a novel strategy for tailoring catalysts to acquire the desired ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion products. [124].
Quantum dots (QDs) have displayed excellent potential in photocatalysis due to their remarkable light absorption ability and matched band gap with MOFs, however, the photoactivity is often limited by radiative recombination of the charges. [3,100] Ding and co-workers made a novel attempt at the synthesis of a nanocomposite, Zn-Bim-His- $1@\mathrm{GQDs}$ in which graphene quantum dots (GQDs) are homogeneously distributed on the surface of MOF by a wet chemical process in room temperature. [109] The homogeneous distribution of GQDs was achieved by the deliberate structural defects on the surface of MOFs. The hybrid material exhibited remarkable characteristics like enhanced photogenerated charge carrier transfer efficiency, notable photostability under repeated cycles of photoreaction, and significant photocatalytic activity owing to the defective porous structure and highly matched energy band of the heterostructure. Yuan et al. employed a sequential deposition route to prepare a series of composite photocatalysts, $\mathrm{MAPbI_{3}@P C N-221(F e_{x})}$ $\mathbf{\left(x=0-1\right.}$ ), wherein lead halide perovskite (LHP) QDs of $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ $(\mathrm{{MAPbl}}_{3}) $ ) were immobilized in the pores of PCN-221 $\left(\mathrm{Fe_{x}}\right)$ MOF. [125] The experimental results revealed that the framework of the MOF substantially enhanced the stability of the LHP QDs, and the minimal distance between the LHP QDs and the catalytic active center in the MOF markedly improved the photo-induced charge separation efficiency. The visible-light-driven ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ photoreduction experiment showed that MAPbI3@PCN-221 $(\mathrm{Fe}_{0.2})$ exhibited the highest photocatalytic activity, producing a yield of $104\upmu\mathrm{mol}\upmu^{-1}$ of CO and $325\upmu\mathrm{mol}\upmu^{-1}$ of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ .
Zhao et al. synthesized an exemplary photocatalyst, ${\mathrm{rGO@NH_{2}}}$ - MIL-125, which produced a notable yield of $1,116\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ of methyl formate. [50] The UV light-driven photoreduction reaction was carried out in a catalyst-methanol slurry system, using hypotoxic methanol as the reducing agent, at ambient temperature and atmospheric pressure. The synergistic effect of rGO incorporation and amino functionality greatly enhanced the photoactivity due to the efficient separation and transfer of photoinduced carriers. Wang et al. employed a MW-assisted in situ assembly technique to load $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ nanocrystals on graphene. [126] The asprepared UiO-66- ${\sf N H}_{2}/{\sf G R}$ is believed to possess ‘super hot spots’ (SHS) which resulted in high dispersion of the nanocrystals on the graphene surface and better interactions between them through tight junctions. Under visible-light irradiation, the prepared catalysts exhibited better photoreduction performance for formic acid formation than those prepared via the conventional hydrothermal method.
Halide perovskite materials are well known for their outstanding light absorption ability and have been effectively employed in optoelectronic applications like solar cells. Their potential in photocatalytic activity has been researched, recently. Wan et al. developed a halide perovskite QDs/MOF nanojunction, $\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}$ $\mathsf{Q D s}/$ $\mathsf{U i O-}66(\mathsf{N H}_{2})$ , which displayed features like high specific surface area, enhanced electron extraction and transfer, excellent photostability and reusability and, high selectivity for CO formation. [127] Although the poor stability of the $\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}$ QDs in the presence of polar solvent or moisture is a matter of concern, coupling the advantages of MOFs with the remarkable optoelectronic properties of $\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}$ QDs, successful photocatalytic conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to valuable fuels in nonaqueous media can be achieved. Low ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capturing capacity and severe radiative recombination are other deterrents of halide perovskites in photocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion. As presented by Kong and coworkers, developing halide perovskites@MOF photocatalysts can bring about an increase in moisture stability, ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption ability, and effective charge separation by synergetic effect. [128] The as-prepared ${\mathsf{C s P b B r}}_{3}@\cdot$ - ZIF-8 and $\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}@\mathsf{Z I F}67$ displayed a 1.39 and 2.66 times increase in electron consumption rate, respectively, as compared to that of the pristine $\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}$ , and effectively converted ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ (with higher selectivity over CO) under visible light irradiation.
Recent research has revealed that the use of membrane catalysts helps overcome the limitations of powder catalysts like aggregation, difficulty in recovery of particles after reaction, and light scattering. [129–130] Furthermore, they provide the added advantages of functionality, stability, and mechanical support. In this context, Zhao et al. synthesized CdS $/\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -UiO-66 hybrid membrane photocatalysts, employing chitosan as the membrane matrix. [129] The membrane catalyst showed high photocatalytic activity and selectivity $(99\mathrm{~\%~})$ , which could be ascribed to the improved ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption ability of chitosan due to excessive amine and hydroxyl groups. In another study, Maina et al. employed the rapid thermal deposition (RTD) method to form hybrid membrane reactors of semiconductor nanoparticles and MOFs. They demonstrated the synthesis of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ and Cu$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ membrane photocatalysts by the deposition of a thin film of semiconductor nanoparticle-doped ZIF-8 on a presynthesized ZIF-8 membrane. Under UV irradiation, $\mathsf{C u T i O}_{2}/\mathsf{Z I F}{-}\mathsf{8}$ with
$7~\upmu\mathrm{g}$ of $\mathsf{C u T i O}_{2}$ NP loading displayed an outstanding photoreduction performance, producing substantially higher yields than the pure ZIF-8 membrane by $233\%$ for CO and $70\%$ for methanol. [131].
Single-atom catalysts help achieve more insights into heterogeneous catalysis at the molecular level. Zhang, Wei, and coworkers incorporated coordinatively unsaturated Co atoms in the MOF525 matrix. [132] This single-atom implanted MOF exhibited remarkable visible-light-driven ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ photoreduction efficiency for CO and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ formation, which can be attributed to the high ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption ability and efficient directional migration of photoinduced electrons to catalytically active Co centers. Lee et al. reported a novel photocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction system, prepared by incorporating $\mathbf{Cr(III)}$ and ${\cal G a}(\mathrm{III})$ to a catechol-functionalized organic linker, catbdc (2,3-dihydroxyterephthalic acid) sites of a post synthetically modified UiO-66 MOF. [133] Under visiblelight irradiation, this photocatalytic system could efficiently produce formic acid in the presence of 1-benzyl-1,4- dihydronicotinamide (BNAH; acting as reductant) and triethanolamine (TEOA; acting as sacrificial agent). The catbdc linkers served as light-harvesting units and the metal-monocatecholato species rendered the efficient transfer of electrons. Owing to the open-shell electronic configuration in $\mathbf{Cr(III)}$ $([\mathsf{A r}]3\mathsf{d}^{3})$ as opposed to the closed-shell electronic configuration in Ga(III) $([\mathsf{A r}]3\mathsf{d}^{10})$ , UiO-66-CrCAT exhibited better photocatalytic performance.
Very recently, Zhao et al. fabricated a 2D ${\mathrm{Zn-MOF/BiVO_{4}}}$ Sscheme heterojunction for the efficient conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to solar fuels. [134] The optimal sample exhibited a 22-fold enhanced photoactivity as compared to $\mathrm{\DeltaBiVO_{4}}$ nanoflakes previously reported in the literature. The well-dispersed $Z_{\mathrm{{n_{2}}(C O O)_{4}}}$ metal nodes in the Zn-MOF function as the catalytically active sites and fostered impressive $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ activation and accelerated the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ conversion process.
MOF derivatives: By utilizing MOFs as templates or precursors, MOFs can be transformed into unique nanostructures in a wellregulated manner. [1,100] The ensuing MOF derivatives possess new well-dispersed catalytically active sites, improved stability, and higher surface area, besides inheriting the pore characteristics of the MOF. [1,66,135–136] Using an Indium-based MOF (MIL-68) hexagonal prism as the precursor, Lou et al. successfully synthesized hierarchical $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}–\mathrm{CdIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ heterostructured nanotubes (Fig. 18). [137] These hierarchical tubular heterostructures displayed striking properties such as a high surface area for enhanced adsorption and concentration of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ molecules, reduced diffusion length for effective separation and migration of photogenerated charge carriers, and profuse catalytically active sites for the redox reactions, and exceptional stability. The HKUST-1 MOF-based three-component-heterojunction photocatalyst, $\mathsf{C}\mathsf{-C u}_{2-\times}\mathsf{S@g-C_{3}N_{4}}$ prepared by Ying et al., displayed excellent photocatalytic activity, primarily due to the high light-absorbing ability of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}\mathtt{S}$ coupled with the efficient charge separation facilitated by the carbon layers. [53] The visible-light-driven ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction under $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ vapor conditions revealed that the composite is a low-cost, highly efficient catalyst, achieving a yield of $1062.6~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ CO with the selectivity of $97\ \%$ , outperforming the majority of the reported $\mathtt{C u}_{2}S$ or $\mathsf{g}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ composites. Chen et al. carried out calcination of MOF nanosheet templates in the air to prepare ultrathin twodimensional porous $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ catalysts. The calcined $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanosheets retained the morphology of 2D and high porosity which enabled electron transport, enhanced ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption, and provided numerous catalytic sites for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ activation, on account of the structural nature of MOF precursors. Consequently, $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{-}$ NS displayed an outstanding photocatalytic activity for the conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to CO. [138] Table 2 summarizes all of the MOFs-based photocatalysts mentioned in this section, highlighting their features.
## 4.2. Electrocatalytic $C O_{2}$ reduction
The electrocatalytic reduction of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to value-added compounds is a tempting approach for lowering the atmospheric ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ level while storing energy. [147] The electroreduction process is economical, sustainable and produces the same end products as the conventional methods, and facilitates net ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and waste reduction. [148–149] In this method, a great variety of electrodes, electrolytes, catalysts, and wide ranges of temperature, pressure, and current density have been investigated to achieve good prospects for the conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to different compounds, mainly CO, also $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , formate, acetate, $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ , $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{5}\mathsf{O H}$ among others. [150–154].
The key attributes of employing electroreduction over other conventional reduction methods are: (1) Easy separation of reaction products, (2) Reactions may be undertaken at ambient temperature and atmospheric pressure, which avoid accidental failures that may occur while raising the temperature and pressure (3) Provides optimum rate of reaction and catalyst selectivity along with recyclable catalyst and electrolyte, and (4) Its reaction system is modular which is not only trouble-free for scale-up application but also economical and sustainable. [100,155–156].
The electrochemical process, involves sequential interactions between the solid electrode, and electrolyte, among products or reactants and revolves around the selectivity of the electrocatalysts. For determining the performance of an electrochemical process, the supreme factors are the rate of formation of product per unit cathode area and the Faradaic efficiency (FE), which are evaluated by the equation:
$$
F E~(\%)~=~(z n F)/q~\times100
$$
where $z$ is the number of electrons exchanged for product formation, n is the number of moles, F is the Faradaic constant $(96485C~\mathrm{mol^{-1}}$ ) and q is the total applied charge. [157].
Nanoporous structured electrocatalysts with exceptionally large surface area, schematic charge transport, and ample active sites, are generally advised to produce products at intensified rate. From an electrochemical perspective, various catalysts have been explored including metal-based, nanostructured, metal-complex, carbon nanotubes, carbon–metal nanocomposites, MOFs, and MOF derivatives/composites, to analyze the multi-functionality, selectivity, and efficiency of the catalyst. [156,158] Limited electrical conductivity, active site obstruction, as well as the unavoidable diffusion barrier of gas, restricts the use of pristine MOFs in electrocatalysis. To overcome this limitation, materials that allow mass transport, accessibility, and highly selective active centers for amplified catalytic activity have been studied.
MOFs with active metal nodes: Although MOFs function as novel catalytic materials for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction owing to their fascinating properties, the lower current density limits their application. In a study by Jiang et al., different ZIFs including ZIF-8, ZIF-108, ZIF-7, and SIM-1 were analyzed for electroreduction, as depicted in Fig. 19; among them, ZIF-8 showed the highest FE $(81.0\ \%)$ at $-1.1\mathrm{~V~}$ vs RHE. [159].
In 2019, Wu and co-workers synthesized a series of ultrathin 2D Ni-MOF nanosheets with contrasting thicknesses. Among them, 2- D $\mathsf{N i}(\mathrm{Im})_{2}$ , a $5\mathrm{nm}$ thick nanosheet, displayed the highest catalytic selectivity, enhanced turnover frequency (TOF), and provided faster reaction kinetics. [160] The active site density of $1.68\times10^{-7}$ mol $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ differentiated this nanosheet from the others, with an FE of $78.8\%$ for CO production.
MOF composites: Numerous studies have been conducted to develop metallic heterogeneous electrocatalysts. In this context, Cu nanoparticles are indeed tempting due to their ability to produce a variety of hydrocarbons as these nanocatalysts play a crucial role in electrocatalysis for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction. Kung et al. presented the first MOF ( $\mathrm{cu}$ -SIM NU-1000) embedding metallic Cu by utilizing the solvothermal deposition in MOFs (SIM) strategy. [161] More recent exploration suggests that separate Cu nanoparticles are far more effective than Cu alloys for C1 product formation mainly methane. Furthermore, frequent accumulation of Cu NPs causes heavy concentration and eventually boosts $C2$ and C3 product formation.
In the year 2018, an unprecedented bimetallic atomically dispersed Co composite $({\mathsf{C o}}/{\mathsf{Z n@Z I F S}})$ was prepared at room temperature by Wang and co-workers. This composite displayed high selectivity with a faradaic efficiency of $94\%$ and a current density of $18.1~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at an overpotential of $520~\mathrm{mV}$ for CO formation, with 18,200 per hour as the turnover frequency. It was also demonstrated that the activation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ was enhanced by lowering the coordination number of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to $\mathsf{C O}_{2}^{\bullet-}$ intermediate. [162] In an attempt to investigate thin films of MOFs on AuNMEs to form MOF/AuNME composite, Luna et al. presented the first report of improved thin-film MOFs on nanostructured metal catalysts for deployment in electrocatalytic tandem reaction. [163] Herein, the nanocomposites were synthesized via layer-by-layer and solvothermal deposition methods, as shown in Fig. 20. Among these, the nanocomposites prepared by the former method contributed to a better yield of CO with $18\%$ FE at $-0.5\mathrm{V}$ vs RHE (Reversible Hydrogen Electrode). Performance according to the principle of tandem catalysis, ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ was first converted to CO on the surface of gold and then further reduced to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{4}$ in the pores of the MOF.
The rival of the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) at reduction electrode potential remains the main constraint in electroreduction catalysis, along with poor selectivity of products formed. In this context, a detailed study was conducted by Guntern et al. to synthesize a nanocrystal (NC)/MOF hybrid, wherein Ag@Al-PMOF $\boldsymbol{\left(\mathrm{PMOF}=\right.}$ porphyrin MOF) showed severe suppression in HER with higher stability when compared with pure Ag NCs. [164] Guo and coworkers were able to develop a hierarchical nanoporous catalyst using the ellipsoidal morphology of MOF and highlighted the benefits of micropores and interconnected mesopores. The composite, TGP-H-060, achieved $98\%$ FE at $-0.6\:\mathrm{V}$ vs RHE which delivers CO as the product at $100\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ for $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ consecutively. [165].
In a study by Zhang et al., a Cu GNC-VL composite was prepared by proper vertical intended design layering of ZIF-L on graphene oxide (GO) nanosheet, accompanied by carbonization of substituted $\mathtt{C u}$ precursor for consecutive $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $1000~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}.$ . This indeed was a notable move, as the above-mentioned composite provided a magnificent number of binding sites with excellent electrical conductivity, for the conversion of atmospheric ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{5}\mathsf{O H}$ compound at a current density of $10.4\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ with extraordinary FE of $70.52\%$ in presence of 0.5 M ${\mathrm{KHCO}}_{3}$ solution. [166].
Xin et al. used a versatile chemical vapor deposition method to prepare MOF composites that exhibit excellent ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ electroreduction activity. They implanted various metal centers of $\mathsf{M C p}_{2}$ $\mathrm{TM}=\mathrm{Fe}$ , Co, Ni) in the pores of metalloporphyrin-based MOF-54 to obtain $\mathrm{MCp}_{2}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}545$ composites (Fig. 21). Herein, the pelectron system of the cyclopentadienyl ring overlap with the pelectron system of porphyrin, entrusting MOF-545 with great ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption capability with an increased number of active sites and favorable electron transfer ability, resulting in an improvement in electrocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction activity. In particular, ${\mathsf{C o C p}}_{2}@{\mathrm{MOF}}{-}545$ showed high faradaic efficiency of $97\%$ at $-0.7\:\mathrm{V}$ vs RHE for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion to CO which is superior to most of the reported MOFs. [167].
As the adsorption capacity and the activation of the metallic framework have a significant impact on electrocatalysis, in order to construct a catalyst that converts ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to a high-value product with high activity and selectivity, a series of $\mathsf{C u}_{2}\mathsf{O}/\mathsf{C u}$ -based nanoparticles were developed by Li and coworkers by carbonizing Cu-btc at various temperatures. Among the prepared catalysts, $\mathsf{C u}_{2}\mathsf{O}/\mathsf{C u}\ @\mathsf{N C}\cdot800$ exhibited formate formation with a maximum FE of $70.5\ \%$ at $-0.68{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs RHE. Although the improved performance was stable for $>30\mathrm{~h~}$ , its limited surface area remains a constraint [168] which was addressed by $\mathtt{Y u}$ et al. via an in-situ etching strategy to restructure $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ , constructing increased specific surface area $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0@\mathtt{C u}$ -MOF composite (Fig. 22) with decent selectivity as well as enhanced efficiency for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ electroreduction to hydrocarbons. [169] The nanocatalyst exhibited a timeresponsive behavior and the surface area increased from $44~\mathrm{m}^{2}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ to $1467~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for the hybrid catalyst. The overall FE for hydrocarbon was $79.4\ \%$ and the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ FE, in particular, was found to be $63.2\ \%$ at $-1.71\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ .
Recently, single-atom catalysts (SACs) have been explored for their utilization in electrocatalysis wherein even a slight change in preparation technique can result in improved catalytic tendency. Keeping this in mind, Gong et al. have successfully proposed the host–guest cooperative approach using in-situ polymerization for SACs to prepare NiSA- $\cdot\Nu_{\mathrm{x}}$ -C/PPy@MgNi-MOF-74 composite; SACs are optimal for the investigation of the organized properties in different reaction conditions. [170] Herein, the single Ni atoms were well organized by adjusting the pyrolysis temperature to showcase their exceptional selectivity, surpassing the performance of the conventional Ni center composites. In another study, Zhao et al. used the previously mentioned single Ni catalyst to construct Ni $S A s/\Nu{-}C@Z\I\r{-}8$ ( $\begin{array}{r}{S{\sf A}s=}\end{array}$ single atoms) composite by utilizing the ion exchange modification technique to selectively amplify the branching of $Z\mathrm{n}$ and Ni ions (Fig. 23). [171] The aforementioned single atom nano-composite showed an incomparable turnover frequency of 5273 per hour for CO preparation with $71.9\%$ FE at a potential of $0.89\mathrm{V}$ and current density $(10.48~\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}.$ ).
In a very recent study, Dong and his group incorporated fullerene molecule $(C_{60})$ into the MOF-545-Co structure by a one-pot impregnation method. [172] The ensuing composite had superior electron transfer ability, ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption capacity, and high selectivity for CO with a FE of $97\%$ at $-0.7\mathrm{~V~}_{;}$ , primarily facilitated by the $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions between the $C_{60}$ and porphyrin systems. Remarkably, the charge transfer resistance $(\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{ct}})$ of the composite is about 5 times less than that of MOF-545-Co and a 1.5-fold increase in ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption capability was observed as compared to the pristine MOF.
MOF derivatives: Yan et al. investigated ZIF-derivative carbon doped with Fe-N sites (C-Fe30-N30/30) for CO production. It was observed that the addition of ammonia to the composite increased the specific surface area, apparently maximizing the exposure of Fe-N active sites for mass transfer during the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction reaction. [173] Kim et al. utilized a hydrothermal strategy for preparing Cu-MOF-74 precursor which on electroreduction develops Cu NP cluster (Fig. 24). The obtained MOF-based Cu NPs favor remarkable electrocatalytic activity for the preparation of C1 products with minimal aggregation. [174] A cathodized nanosheet of MOF was employed by Yang and co-workers as they reported $\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{II})/$ adeninoto/carboxylate MOFs $(\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{II}),$ /ade-MOF), exhibiting remarkable electrocatalytic conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to ethylene with $45\%$ FE and $8.5\ \mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ current density at $-1.4\mathrm{~V~}$ vs RHE, while $50\%$ FE and $15\mathrm{\mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ current density at $-1.6{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs RHE for methane. [175] Lamogni and co-workers revealed the implementation of Bi-MOF in an unprecedented ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ electroreduction wherein efficient formate formation was reported using Bi-based nanoparticles prepared following the structural design of Bi(btb) (btb $\L_{1}=$ (Bi(1,3,5- tris(4-carboxyphenyl)benzene)) framework. Herein, the preferential conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to formate was demonstrated with $95\%$ FE at $770~\mathrm{mV}$ overpotential. [176] Yet in another study, the thermal decomposition technique was used to incorporate Bi-N4 on carbon framework to synthesize ordered porous Bi SAs/NC electrocatalyst which showed a remarkable yield of CO (FE $97\%$ ) at a lower overpotential of $-0.5\mathrm{\:V}$ vs RHE with $-3.9\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ as current density. [177].
Chen et al. successfully demonstrated the relationship between voids and the electrocatalytic activity of the composites by preparing a novel FeSAs/CNF-900 composite exploiting the Kirkendall effect during the pyrolysis process (Fig. 25 A). Their study revealed that SACs can be synthesized very effectively by using suitable means with desired and controlled morphology. [178] $\mathrm{FeSAs}/$ CNF-900 displayed a turnover frequency of 639.9 per hour for CO production with up to $86.9\%\mathrm{FE}$ at $-0.47\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ . The presence of ample large mesopores $\cdot\sim10\ \mathrm{nm})$ in the porous carbon nanoframeworks enhanced the mass transport and made the well-dispersed Fe- $\cdot\Nu_{\mathrm{x}}$ sites easily reachable to the reactants. The integration of heteroatoms such as nitrogen, sulphur, phosphorus, etc. into the carbon composition, plays an incredibly vital role in strengthening the fundamental characteristics of carbon materials that are responsible for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction efficiency. Wang et al. utilized $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped carbon nanotubes (CNTs) and ZIF-8 framework to prepare $\mathsf{N C-900}/$
ZIF-8 composite which displayed high selectivity of $78\%C O$ production (Fig. 25 B). [179] It was found that the catalytic performance was dependent on the pyrolysis temperature and the high activity could be ascribed to the existence of pyridinic-N and quarternary-N species in abundance in the carbon structure.
The bottleneck of lower current density in electrocatalysis was addressed by Sun et al. by deploying MoP@In-PC composite in ionic liquid, 1-butyl-3-methylimidazolium hexafluorophosphate, as the supporting electrolyte when a notable yield of formic acid was observed with FE and a current density of $96.5\%$ and $43.8\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , respectively. [180] It is worth noting that to attain unprecedented higher catalytic efficiency for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction reaction $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ RR), Zhang et al. revealed the use of metal doping on the framework to prepare composite (Fig. 26 a); a highly selective $\mathsf{A g}/\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}\mathsf{-}$ MOFs composite was generated with $55.6\ \%$ faradaic efficiency for CO production. [181] The rate of CO production could be regulated by varying the potential values, i.e, CO production increased with the rise in potential. The catalyst retained stability for ${>}10\mathrm{h}$ at $-1.8{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs SCE. Silver $(\mathsf{A g})$ catalysts have shown considerable prospects in electrocatalysis, owing to their exceptional tunable properties, however, their activity is typically restricted by the limited availability of surface area for mass transportation. Jiang and coworkers employed a one-pot hydrothermal route to prepare an $\mathsf{A g}_{2}\mathsf{O}/$ layered ZIF composite, which displayed increased efficiency for CO production overwhelming the activity of layered ZIF or $\mathsf{A g/C}$ . [182] Recently, another approach of reticular chemistry was introduced by Nam et al. to tune the metal catalysts wherein a modified Ag/Zr-FCU-MOF-NDC (FCU $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ face-centered cubic, ${\mathsf{N D C}}=1{,}4$ -naphthalenedicarboxylic acid) electrocatalyst was produced with enhanced binding capability by diverging the linker and nodes of fcc MOF (Fig. 26 b). This strategy has intensified the CO selectivity to $94\%$ under fixed conditions. [183] Authors were successful in preventing the binding of reaction intermediates and increased the local concentration of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ near the $\mathsf{A g}$ nanoparticles. This was possible by introducing amine functionality and altering the organic linker and metal node. The enhanced ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption, tunable porosity, and lewis acidity contributed significantly to the improved catalytic activity.
In a study by Zheng et al., an $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped carbon catalyst was prepared using calcined ZIF-8 and the ensued composite displayed $95.4\ \%$ FE for CO production at $-0.5\mathrm{~V~}$ vs RHE; pyrolysis at higher temperatures showed enhanced catalytic activity. Also, calculation by density functional theory (DFT) revealed the minimization of overpotential caused via the presence of numerous pyridinic N catalysts. [184] By refining the previously mentioned $N.$ -doped carbon with a single atom of $\mathtt{C u}$ $4.9\%$ mol concentration), Guan et al. successfully established the significance of separation amongst Cu atomic sites for developing a hydrocarbon-based composite for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction. [185] In another study, cathodized 2-D Cu MOF nanosheets have been explored by Wu and his co-workers as they reconstructed the cathodized Cu-based nanosheet, which on XRD, XPS, FT-IR, as well as HRTEM investigation, exhibited remarkable productivity. Their observation revealed that the restructuring of cathodized Cu porphyrin MOF nanosheet, CuO, $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ , Cu, and a porphyrin- $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ complex (CuTCPP) complements were prepared, which favor the formation of formate and acetate (Fig. 27). [186].
Ye and co-workers used a post-synthetic modification technique to incarcerate ammonium ferric citrate (AFC) on ZIF-8 surface to form C-AFC@ZIF-8. On pyrolysis, isolated Fe-N sites were generated, which enhanced the catalytic performance of the composite to produce CO with $93\ \%\ \mathrm{FE}$ at $0.33~\mathrm{V}$ vs RHE using $1\mathrm{~M~}$ ${\mathrm{KHCO}}_{3}$ as an electrode and current efficiency of $0.724{\mathrm{~mA~cm}}^{-2}$ . [187] The deficiency of surface area in ZIF-8 has been a lingering challenge that needs to be circumvented. In this regard, Hu et al. presented a strategy to protectively coat the ZIF-8 surface using $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ which enhances the specific surface area to generate porous, Fe-ZIF $\varpi\mathsf{S i O}_{2}$ composite that exhibited improved CO formation FE from $75\%$ to $99\%$ in $1\ \mathrm{mol/L}\ \mathrm{KHCO}_{3}$ solution. [188].
In a recent study by Yang and co-workers, a polycrystalline nanocatalyst was prepared by a controllable annealing process wherein the $\mathtt{C u O}$ nanowire demonstrated a good yield of $C2$ products $\mathrm{\dot{C}_{2}H_{4}}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CH}_{2}0\mathrm{H})$ at $-1.3{\mathrm{~V~}}$ vs RHE with a maximum FE of $\sim70\ \%$ and prompted future research on the development of outstanding MOF-materials. [189] Kongi and coworkers synthesized a series of Bi-based electrocatalysts by carbonization of
TAL-33 MOF precursor at different temperatures. [190] The ensuing catalysts displayed high activity and selectivity for formate production, with faradaic efficiencies varying between $51\%$ and $100\%$ upon $1.5\mathrm{~h~}$ of testing. Experimental results indicated that the formation of bismuth nanoparticles at high temperatures rendered the highest efficiency and stability for the catalyst carbonized at $600~^{\circ}C$ (FE of $100\%$ which was reduced only by a mere $6\%$ after $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ ).
Table 3 summarizes all of the MOFs-based electrocatalysts mentioned in this section, highlighting their features.
## 4.3. Photoelectrocatalytic $C O_{2}$ reduction
Poor separation of photoinduced charge carriers is the major hindrance in photocatalysis while high temperature and voltage conditions and low selectivity are the impediments associated with electrocatalysis. [200] Photoelectrocatalysis (PEC) integrates the merits of photocatalysis and electrocatalysis while surmounting their individual limitations (Fig. 28). A photoelectrochemical cell consists of a semiconductor photoelectrode, another electrode, and an electrolyte. Radiations with energy higher than the bandgap energy of the photoelectrode generate electron-hole pairs which can be separated by a potential gradient at the interface between the photoelectrode and the electrolyte. [201] The photoinduced electrons at the surface of the photocatalyst are involved in the reduction of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to various valuable chemicals. [4].
Photoelectrocatalysis using MOF catalysts is an emerging technology and is in its nascent stage. The first reported MOF-based photoelectrocatalyst was prepared by decorating thin films of ZIF-8 on $\mathrm{Ti}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanotube electrodes (Fig. 29). [13] ZIF-8 was particularly chosen due to its outstanding stability, large surface area, and remarkably selective $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ adsorption and activation capacity. The MOF also functioned as a co-catalyst for facilitating the migration of photoexcited electrons. Under UV–vis irradiation, the $\mathrm{Ti}/$ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\mathrm{NT}$ -ZIF-8 ( $\mathbf{\partial}\cdot\mathbf{NT}=\mathbf{\partial}$ nanotube) electrodes reduced ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ in aqueous media to methanol $(0.7~\mathrm{mmol/L})$ and ethanol $(10\ \mathrm{mmol/L})$ at an applied potential of $0.1\mathrm{V}.$ . In another work, Irikura et al. employed a solvothermal method to grow $\mathsf{R u}_{3}(\mathsf{B T C})_{2}$ MOF films on $\mathrm{Ti}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\mathrm{NT}$ electrode. The integration of thin films of the MOF enhanced the visible light absorption, augmented the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption in aqueous media, and restricted the recombination of photoexcited electronhole pairs. The ensuing $\mathrm{Ti}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\mathrm{NT}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Ru}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ electrode gave a yield of $314~{\upmu\mathrm{mol/L}}$ of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ . [202] Cheng et al. synthesized $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{B T C})$ - rGO via a hydrothermal synthetic route and employed it as a cathode catalyst in a photoelectrochemical cell for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion to ethanol. [203] Experimental analyses revealed a quick transfer of electrons on the catalyst surface and also the presence of more number of active sites. At an applied voltage of $-2\mathsf{V}$ , the conversion rate of the carbon atom was found to reach 3256 nmol $\ensuremath{\mathbf{h}}^{-1}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , with a selectivity of $94.6~\%$ for ethanol.
Since the electrical conductivity and chemical and thermal stability of pristine MOFs are not up to the mark, these aspects can be improved by carbonization under inert conditions to form hierarchical porous hybrid carbon, possessing a higher number of active sites and an assortment of micropores and mesopores, which favor superior catalytic performance. [204–205] Most often, the catalysts are immobilized on conductive supports with the help of polymer adhesives like Nafion glue. But this technique comes with the inherent drawbacks of the possibility of blockage of pores and agglomeration of the powder catalyst. [11,205] Addressing these issues, Maina et al. directly carbonized porous metal-supported MOF hybrid membranes, thereby triumphantly fabricating hybrid carbon photoelectrocatalytic reactors on the macroporous metal substrate. [11] They analyzed the catalytic performance of the hybrid catalysts as a function of carbonization temperature and precursor MOF membrane composition and found that the samples carbonized at $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ manifested the best catalytic activity, producing methanol yield 9-fold as that of noncarbonized MOFs.
Deng and co-workers employed a facile in-situ method for coating the surface of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ photocathode with $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{B T C})_{2}$ MOF (HKUST-1) for the photoelectrocatalytic conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to CO in acetonitrile. [206] In spite of its noteworthy features such as low toxicity, ease of availability, and ample visible-light absorption, $\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ lacks photostability and the catalytically active Cu sites undergo chemical changes when an external potential is applied. Coating this photocathode with MOF not only protects it from photo corrosion but also provides more active $\mathtt{C u}$ sites for catalytic reduction of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . The ultrafast spectroscopy revealed that the interface seamlessly facilitated charge separation and migration. Zanoni et al. electrochemically prepared $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{2}0{-}\mathsf{C u}$ (BDC) electrode by anodic deposition of $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{B D C})$ thin films on Cu electrode (Fig. 30 a). The current density, temperature, and electrodeposition time were assessed for their influence on the thickness of the films, photoactivity, and stability. The electrode prepared under a current density of $2.5\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ for $6.5\ \mathrm{min}$ at $110\mathrm{~\textdegreeC}$ exhibited the highest catalytic activity. Under UV–vis irradiation, $234~{\upmu\mathrm{mol/L}}$ of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ was produced in $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ at an applied voltage of $0.1\mathrm{~V~}$ , which is $\sim20$ times more than that formed using an unmodified $\mathtt{C u}/\mathtt{C u}_{2}0$ electrode. [207] In another study, aminofunctionalized MOF, $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{B D C-N H_{2}})$ ) was electrochemically deposited on $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{C u}_{2}0$ electrode (Fig. $30\ \mathsf{b}$ ), which produced a significantly better yield of methanol $(674\ \upmu\mathrm{mol/L})$ under identical reaction conditions. The enhanced catalytic performance can be ascribed to the improved ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption due to the introduction of an amino group. [208].
Very recently, Ren et al. employed an in situ synthesis to embed ZIF-8 particles into ${\tt B i}_{2}{S}_{3}$ to form a heterojunction structure that could convert ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to formate photoelectrochemically. [134] On analyzing the catalytic efficiency of the composites of different $\mathrm{Bi}/\mathrm{Zn}$ mole ratios, the catalyst with $1{:}10\ \mathrm{Bi}/Z\mathrm{n\Omega}$ ratio displayed the highest photoelectric catalytic performance and showed no fluctuation in performance in the stability test for up to $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
Table 4 summarizes the recently reported MOF-based photoelectrocatalysts for the conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ .
## 4.4. Catalytic hydrogenation
The addition of one or more hydrogen atoms to any compound in the presence of a catalyst to obtain a distinct compound is termed hydrogenation. Catalytic hydrogenation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ is a widely used method for the conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ into value-added products. The current catalysts have various drawbacks and thus require innovative alternatives. For instance, $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{ZnO}/\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ which is a commonly used industrial catalyst for the conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to methanol gives CO as a byproduct, which raises environmental concerns. [209].
In this section, various MOF-based nanocatalysts deployed for the catalytic hydrogenation to useful compounds are discussed namely the production of methane $\mathrm{(CH_{4})}$ , carbon monoxide (CO), methanol $(\mathrm{CH}_{3}0\mathrm{H})$ , and olefins. Thus, it is a two-for-one method as it not only puts forward an efficient way to curb the rising ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ levels but also offers useful value-added products.
The major parameters deployed to compare these catalysts are ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion rate, product selectivity, Space-Time Yield (STY), and the reaction conditions in which they display catalytic activity.
## 4.4.1. $C O_{2}$ to methanol
Among the aforementioned conversions, hydrogenation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to methanol is the most researched transformation, primarily because of the wide range of methanol usage as a solvent, fuel, and additive to other products. The underlying mechanism for the hydrogenation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to methanol is rather simple and proceeds through a formate intermediate. During this hydrogenation reaction, the formation of carbon monoxide is also observed as a by-product, due to the reverse water–gas shift reaction (Fig. 31). [210].
Zhongjie et al. studied the catalytic activity of Pd supported on MIL-68(In)-derived $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ nanotubes at $295^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ and 30 bar (3 MPa). Although the catalysts like $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{Pd}$ have already been used, this particular combination wherein Pd was loaded on hollow $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ showed superior catalytic activity; $10.5\ \%\ C O_{2}$ conversion and $72.4\%$ methanol selectivity were attained along with a space–time yield of $0.53~\mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{MeOH}}\mathrm{h}^{-1}~\mathrm{g}~\mathrm{cat}^{-1}$ over $^{100\mathrm{~h~}}$ [212].
In another study, hydrogenation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to methanol using ultrasmall $\mathrm{{Cu}/\mathrm{{ZnO_{x}}}}$ nanoparticles confined in MOF gave a remarkable selectivity of $100\%$ along with a space–time yield of up to 2.59 gMeOH $\mathrm{kg}_{\mathrm{Cu}}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ which is much higher than that of the commercial $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{ZnO}/\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ catalyst $(0.83\mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{MeOH}}\mathrm{kg}_{\mathrm{Cu}}^{-1}\mathrm{h}^{-1})$ . Moreover, the stability tests for the catalysts, which were run for $100\mathrm{~h~}$ , showed that the product selectivity remained fairly stable and the activity only reduced by a small margin, indicating excellent catalytic stability. [213] Meanwhile, the same $\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{Z n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ nanoparticles affixed with Zeolite $\{\mathsf{C u}/\mathsf{Z n O}_{\mathsf{x}}(1.38)@\mathsf{N a}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{Z S M}\mathrm{-}5\}$ showed a higher space–time yield of $44.88\ \mathrm{\g}_{\mathrm{MeOH}}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}\mathrm{Cu}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ with $71\%$ methanol selectivity [214] with improved catalytic performance and superior durability due to its secured structure.
In the study of catalytic performance of Uio-67-Pt, wherein Pt nanoparticles occupied the cavities of UiO-67, $18\%$ methanol selectivity was reported for this MOF-based catalyst at $170^{\circ}\mathsf C$ temperature and 1–8 bar pressure; interestingly, the MOF structure of Uio-67-Pt was found to be preserved even after continual operations. [210,215] Pustovarenko et al. reported the methanol selectivity of the MOF-based $\mathrm{In}_{2}0_{3}@{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}0_{4}$ catalyst to be $63\%$ and the STY to be $0.55~\mathrm{g}_{\mathrm{MeOH}}{\cdot}\mathrm{gcat}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ at $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and 50 bar pressure. [214] This catalyst can be operated over a wider temperature and pressure window, because of its tunable performance. Structural reorganization is also an important factor at play here, changing $\mathrm{CO}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ to $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathrm{InC}_{0.75}$ with an amorphous $\mathrm{In}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ shell.
Meanwhile, a copper nanocatalyst has been reported which was confined in a $Z\mathrm{{r}(I V)}$ -based MOF, $\mathrm{{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{{O}_{4}(O H)_{4}(B D C)_{6}}}$ $[\mathrm{BDC}=1,4$ -ben zenedicarboxylate] (Fig. 32 a) exhibiting an excellent methanol selectivity $(100\%)$ . [217] The adequate spacing provided by organic linkers ensures that reactants are well supplied to the active sites. Rungtaweevoranit et al. also conducted $\mathsf{X}$ -ray photoelectron spectroscopy studies which proposed that the high activity of the catalyst could be because of the strong interactions between copper nanocatalyst and Zr Oxide SBUs of the MOF. By the integration of Zn-BTC MOF nanoparticles and layered double hydroxide (LDH) nanosheets, Zhao et al. synthesized a series of hierarchical sheetlike $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{Zn}/\mathrm{Al}$ nanocatalysts, as depicted in Fig. 32 (b). The study unveiled that the pretreatment of the LDH with acetone significantly increases the BET surface area, which in turn facilitates improved yield and selectivity $(>90~\%$ at $200^{\circ}\mathsf C$ ) of methanol in ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ hydrogenation. [218].
## 4.4.2. $C O_{2}$ to methane
The reduction of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to methane, also known as the Sabatier reaction, is another efficient method to obtain value-added products from ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . ${\mathsf{N i@M O F}}{\boldsymbol{\cdot}}5$ is one such MOF-based catalyst that displayed excellent product selectivity in ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ methanation. Longterm stability tests (up to $100\mathrm{h}$ ) showed that they exhibit high catalytic stability and almost no deactivation. [219] Lin et al. synthesized MOF-based ${\mathsf{N i@C}}$ catalysts with a unique hollow structure which were remarkably stable and also showcased excellent stability and selectivity for methanation reaction. [220].
MOF-based Ru-impregnated zirconium-based catalysts displayed superior catalytic activity with an outstanding methane selectivity $(99\%)$ and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion rate of $96\%$ ; catalytic activity is dependent on the MOF framework. [221] The study by Zhao et al. on ultrasmall Ni@UiO66 catalysts for the methanation exhibited a $68.2\ \%\ C O_{2}$ conversion and a good $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ selectivity at $320^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , which can be attributed to the highly monodisperse and ultrasmall nature of the catalyst. [222] $20\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{MIL}-101$ catalyst generated through the Double Solvent Method (DSM) not only exhibited good product selectivity but also superior thermal stability and TOF. As the temperature increased over $200~^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ , a rapid increase in activity was noticed. [223].
The methanation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ could have two possible mechanisms; one that involves the formation of a CO intermediate and another where ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ is directly hydrogenated to methane (Fig. 33). Conclusions drawn by Zhen et al., based on their studies, suggest that the first pathway is usually followed, where an intermediate carbon monoxide is formed. [224].
## 4.4.3. $C O_{2}$ to olefins
Light olefins $(C_{2^{-}}C_{4})$ are another group of value-added compounds which function as building blocks for many plastic products. Ramirez et al. reported an iron-based catalyst $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}0_{3}@\mathsf{K O}_{2}/\mathrm{ZrS}/{\mathsf{S}t}$ APO34 which gave a space–time yield 10.4 mmol gcat- $\cdot^{1}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ at $375^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and 30 bar pressure. [224] It exhibited a decent selectivity and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion which outperformed conventional zeolite-based systems for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversions. Hu et al. reported two MOF-based catalysts, ZIF-8 and MIL-53(Al), for the conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to light olefins. The porous structure of these MOFs increases their surface area and opens the door to a large number of pore dimensions and topologies. The
XRD patterns affirmed their stability at $3\mathrm{MPa}$ pressure and a temperature of $573\ \mathrm{K}.$ Factors like the acidity of supports and the secondary reaction of hydrogenation during internal diffusion additionally influence the product selectivity. [227].
The most common mechanism for the conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to olefins is a direct route wherein conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to CO takes place through Reverse Water Gas Shift Reaction (RSWG) which is accompanied by a classical Fischer-Tropsch synthesis (FTS) mechanism, where the CO is converted to olefins. [228].
## 4.4.4. $C O_{2}$ to carbon monoxide (CO)
Yet another important reaction is the Reverse Water Gas Shift Reaction (RSWG), wherein ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ is hydrogenated to obtain carbon monoxide (CO), which is used for the production of aldehydes, detergents, phosgene, among others. $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{Au}@\mathrm{Pd}@\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{oba})_{4}(3-$ bpdh) $_{2}\mathrm{)}4\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ (1Co) [oba $\mathit{\Omega}=4,4^{\prime}$ -oxybis(benzoic acid); 3-bpdh $\mathbf{\tau}=\mathbf{N}$ , ${\bf N}^{\prime}$ -bis-(1-pyridine-3-yl-ethylidene)-hydrazine] showed good catalytic activity for selective conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to CO. [229] These composites, assembled by solvothermal synthesis, were observed to have a product selectivity of up to $98\%$ and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion of $18.2\%$ . Two organic linkers, oba, and 3-bpdh were used in the synthesis process. Pan et al. synthesized a MOF-based catalyst HZSM$5@\mathrm{UIO-66-NH_{2}/P d}$ which displayed a fairly high ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion of $77.4\%$ . For studies conducted at 20 bar and $320^{\circ}\mathsf C$ CO selectivity was found to be $92.2\ \%$ , making them a promising catalyst for RSWG reactions; [230] small dimensions of Pd nanoparticles apparently facilitated the conversion to CO.
Catalytic hydrogenation to form CO mainly follows two reaction mechanisms or reaction pathways. One of them is a redox mechanism of active metal centers, where reaction proceeds in two steps. $(\mathsf{C O}_{2}+\mathsf{M}^{n+}\to\mathsf{M O}_{\mathrm{x}}+\mathsf{C O}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}+\mathrm{MO}_{\mathrm{x}}\rightarrow\mathrm{M}^{n+}+\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ . The second pathway is also a two-step mechanism where ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ is first hydrogenated into formate, followed by cleavage of the $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bond. This is known as the formate decomposition mechanism. [229].
Table 5 summarizes all of the MOFs-based nanocatalysts for the catalytic hydrogenation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , highlighting their features.
## 5. Organic transformation reactions of $\mathbf{CO_{2}}$
## 5.1. MOFs-catalyzed cycloaddition of $C O_{2}$ and epoxides
As previously mentioned, a huge quantity of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ is emitted into the atmosphere as a direct consequence of human reliance on fossil fuels. An assortment of procedures to reduce the unpropitious global problem, arising due to the burning of fossil fuels is a muchawaited need of today’s era. Thus, an innovative method of the catalytic cycloaddition of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ with epoxides to generate organic cyclic carbonates is perhaps amongst the most successful eco-friendly approaches for the reduction of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ . [233–236] Some of the common epoxides and their corresponding ensued cyclic carbonate after cycloaddition of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ are presented in Table 6.
To synthesize organic cyclic carbonates, which are important intermediates in the chemical and pharmaceutical industries, the cycloaddition of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to epoxides is a superior strategy to other conventional methods. [237] Numerous MOFs and MOFs-based composites have been developed to serve as heterogeneous catalysts for cycloaddition of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ with epoxides, as presented in Table 6. Mainly these composites are divided into different categories depending upon the catalytic sites used for various reactions. Assorted catalytic sites include active catalytic defect/metal sites, bifunctional metal centers, or functional/co-catalyst linkers. [28] Table 7.
The basic mechanism of cycloaddition reaction of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ into epoxides in presence of co-catalyst and Lewis acid catalyst (Fig. 34) involves the following three key steps:
1) Activation of epoxides with Lewis acid catalyst. 2) Formation of halo-alkoxide by the nucleophilic attack of the co-catalyst. 3) The cycloaddition of halo-alkoxide intermediate with ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to form required cyclic carbonate. [238].
The existence of tiny pores in some catalysts hampers their utility for converting the heavier epoxides. To address this challenge, Aguila and co-workers employed the free radical polymerization technique to integrate linear ionic polymer (IP) into the morphology of MOF, MIL-101. Herewith, the MIL-101-IP surpasses the activity of independent species for the cycloaddition of epichlorohydrin with ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , also achieving nearly quantitative conversion of epichlorohydrin and a good yield of products after recycling the composite four times. [239] Although the first reported use of ILs was described in 2005, [237] inducing poly-ionic liquids(imidazolium) into the MIL-101 MOF, via in situ polymerization was offered by Ding et al. in the year 2018; ensuing polyILs@MIL-101 composite demonstrates enhanced $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ capture ability for the cycloaddition reaction of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ with epoxides. The availability of a large surface area and porous morphology of the MIL-101 MOF favors easy access to ILs as well as excellent ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption. Furthermore, the presence of Lewis acid sites of MOF and Lewis base site of polyILs attributes to the comparatively improved performance of the synthesized composite. [70] Another study successfully constructed Salen- $\cdot\mathrm{Cu(II)}@\mathrm{MIL}-101(\mathrm{Cr})$ composite using the ‘‘ship in a bottle” method. The aforementioned catalysts along with tetrabutylammonium bromide co-catalyst demonstrated a high yield of $87.8\ \%$ for the production of propylene carbonate under ambient atmospheric conditions. [240] This study also revealed the enhancement in ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption and cycloaddition conversion capability due to the inclusion of bi-active centers particularly Cr (III) and $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ .
Liang et al. presented another study where reticular chemistry and PSM strategy was used for fabricating bifunctional imidazolium-based Zr-MOF, resulting in the formation of (I-) Meim-UiO-66 composite. [235] Under ambient pressure of 1 atm and a high temperature of $120^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ , the catalyst presents novel effectiveness in the coupling of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ with epoxides, a plausible mechanism being shown in Fig. 35.
The presence of various active sites in MOFs is responsible for the excellent reactivity of epoxides with ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ for the generation of cyclic carbonate. In this context, Kurisingal and co-workers developed a Zn-based multi-faced heterogeneous catalyst represented as MOF-5-MIX by combining multiple functionalized species into the MOFs. [241] This study illustrated the substantial impact of the functionalized ligands on the arrangement of synergistic sites for expediting the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and epoxides cycloaddition reaction. The aforementioned composite outperforms all the previously synthesized catalysts such as MOF-5, MOF- $5\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ , MOF-5-OH, demonstrating excellent $98\%$ ECH conversion at $50^{\circ}C$ for the time duration of $6\mathrm{~h~}$ .
Jiang et al. fabricated a flexible porphyrin-based rtl (rutile) MOF, $[\mathrm{Mn}_{5}\mathrm{L}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}(\mathrm{DMA})_{2}].5\mathrm{DMA}.4\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{5}\mathrm{OH}$ (denoted as 1.Mn) which demonstrated the maximum catalytic activity in the first cycle at 20 atm pressure, with a TON of 800 per mole of composite. [242] The availability of Lewis-acid Mn sites functioned as a good composite for fixing atmospheric ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , showcasing outstanding size selectivity, reactivity, and stability of the porphyrin-based MOF. Sharma and co-workers 2019, in an unprecedented effort, built a 3D supramolecular structure that absorbs visible light. [243] Under ambient temperature and pressure of 1 bar, the as-synthesized Mn (II)-porphyrin composite serve as a reusable catalyst with high activity for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ cycloaddition to epoxides; the plausible mechanism for cyclic carbonate formation is depicted in Fig. 36 (a). Another recent report provided an economical and efficient approach for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ cycloaddition reaction under ambient conditions by introducing light during the reaction procedure. Herein, Zhai et al. presented the incorporation of Bi atom as Lewis acid into the porphyrin ring of a PCN-224 to facilitate the ring-opening structure of propylene oxide. [244] Unlike the usual thermaldriven reaction mechanism, light is injected into the process, enabling the reaction to occur under ambient circumstances, with a vital impact in terms of sustainability.
In a recent study, Liu et al. initially constructed a functionalized metal–organic layer referred to as MTTB MOL, where MTTB means that it was synthesized from TTB $(4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime})_{}-$ s-triazine-2,4,6-triyl-tri benzoate). It was further implanted upon a three-dimensional(3- D) PCN-222(Co) in one step (Fig. $36\ \mathsf{b}$ ); [245] ensuing PCN-222 (Co)@MTTB displayed remarkably enhanced catalytic activity in the cycloaddition process of carbon dioxide $(\mathsf{C O}_{2})$ with epoxides. The outer acidic and basic sites of the frameworks matrix are crucial for the composite’s unique catalytic efficiency, resulting in an established approach for exploring ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction to significant cyclic compounds under mild conditions. Table 6 summarizes all of the MOFs-based composites for cycloaddition of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ with epoxides, highlighting their features.
## 5.2. Carboxylation of terminal alkynes with $C O_{2}$
The carboxylation reaction of terminal alkynes and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ is an effective strategy for the chemical fixation of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ wherein useful carboxylic acids are synthesized via ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{H}}$ bond activation of terminal alkyne molecules. [26] For the synthesis of propiolic acids, carboxylation of terminal alkynes is much more environment-friendly than other alternatives. [3].
Silver $(\mathsf{A g})$ is currently the most preferred metal for the activation of alkynes. Because of its distinctive d [10] configuration, it can easily interact with the $\mathsf{C}{\equiv}\mathsf{C}$ bond, making it one of the most effective activators. [254] The first report on the conversion of terminal alkynes to propionic acid with ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , using Ag@MIL-101 catalyst was offered by Liu et al. in 2015 when they prepared ${\mathsf{A g@M I L-101}}$ catalysts by employing a simple liquid impregnation approach to immobilize Ag NPs in MIL-101(Cr). Ag@MIL-101 displays an exceptional bifunctional character as it can simultaneously capture and convert $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ while consuming a very little amount of energy. [255] Though the as-prepared catalyst showed outstanding catalytic properties, some improvements were still required because of the poisonous nature of $\operatorname{Cr}(\operatorname{III})$ and also due to the harmful health implication of deploying hydrofluoric acid in the synthesis of MIL-101(Cr). [256] Consequently, the same group carried out the capture and transformation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ into alkynyl carboxylic acids by using MIL-100(Fe) and UIO-66(Zr) in place of Ag@MIL-101(Cr) system; MIL-100(Fe) and UIO-66(Zr) are facile to synthesize, exhibit high efficiency for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion, and are more eco-friendly. [256].
Shi et al. devised a facile and expeditious method for the preparation of ${\sf A g@Z I F-8}$ to accomplish the carboxylation of terminal alkynes with ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . [257] They incorporated two types of Ag species; (1) aggregated $\mathtt{A g}(0)$ nanoparticles on the outer surface $(\mathsf{A g}^{\mathsf{N P}})$ Owing to its high accessibility, these ${\mathsf{A g}}^{\mathsf{N P}}$ sites were responsible for efficient activation of the terminal alkynes, (2) highly dispersed $\mathsf{A g}(\mathrm{I})$ in the backbone $(\mathsf{A g}^{\mathrm{HD}})$ ; ${\mathsf{A g}}^{\mathrm{HD}}$ modified ZIF-8 framework resulted in its increased affinity as a ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reservoir. The combination of these two factors led to the higher activity of Ag@ZIF-8 catalyst in the aforementioned carboxylation reaction.
Gong et al. synthesized a novel composite (UiO-66@UiO-67- BPY-Ag), via post-synthetic modification of an active metal species $(\mathsf{A g})$ , coordinated in the shell structure (UiO-67-BPY) of the core– shell (UiO-66) (Fig. 37 a). The composite material was effectively deployed as a catalyst for the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ fixation via carboxylation of terminal alkynes (Fig. 37 b) in a high-performance catalytic reaction under mild conditions wherein it could be recycled and re-used at least 5 times without appreciable loss of the catalytic activity. [254] Excellent catalytic activity of the fabricated material highlighted the superiority of metallated core–shell structure over earlier reported silver catalysts such as encapsulated Ag nanoparticles (AgNP@MOF).
Molla and co-workers embedded AgNPs over a porous Co-MOF to produce a novel type of nanocatalyst, AgNPs/Co-MOF for the carboxylation of terminal alkynes with ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . [258] The synthesized catalytic material displayed high catalytic activity, higher stability in moisture, while utilizing are inexpensive and easily accessible precursors for their synthesis. Moreover, the catalyst AgNPs/CoMOF can be reused for several reaction cycles maintaining a stable catalytic performance. Shi et al. contributed the first report on the employment of a $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ -based MOF, $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I N})$ -MOF as a catalyst for the carboxylation of terminal alkynes with CO. [259] where varying the oxidation state of Cu plays a crucial role in the catalytic mechanism. Cu (IN)-MOF, besides being easy to synthesize, costeffective, and environment-friendly, demonstrates an efficient performance as a heterogeneous catalyst for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion under mild conditions. Wang and co-workers successfully fabricated a robust $[\mathsf{C u}_{4}(\upmu_{3}-(0\mathrm{H})_{2}]$ -cluster-based MOF (Cu-MOF) via the MWassisted method which could effectively catalyze the carboxylation of terminal alkynes with ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ under mild reaction conditions. In addition, this $\mathtt{C u}$ -MOF displays high stability and could be easily recycled five times without considerable loss of catalytic activity. [40].
## 5.3. $C O_{2}$ reductive amination
Reductive amination is another organic transformation that needs to be considered when we study ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion wherein the carbonyl group is usually converted into an amine, through an imine intermediate. Reductive amination offers various advantages including easier synthesis and a high yield. Strotman et al. observed that Transfer Hydrogen Catalyst can be used to optimize the asymmetric reductive amination. [260].
Studies have also been conducted in ultrathin two-dimensional MOF nanosheets based on a halogen-substituted porphyrin ligand which also revealed good catalytic activity for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reductive amination.
## 5.4. $C O_{2}$ hydrosilylation
Hydrosilylation is another method for organic transformation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ which should be studied closely due to its potential as a good catalytic method that essentially entails the addition of Si-H bond to an unsaturated bond, carbonyl in this case.
A metal–organic framework (MOF) containing metal-free Nheterocyclic carbene moieties has been found to be a good tool for hydrosilylation as it provides not only good stability but also enhanced selectivity towards the desired product. [259].
Studies have also been conducted in complex materials like Co (II)-MOF $\{[\mathsf{C O}_{2}(\mathrm{\iota-mac})(4,4\mathrm{-bpt})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})]{\cdot}3{\cdot}5\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\}_{n}.$ , was found to have the ability to promote hydrosilylation of alkynes under harsh conditions with moderate yield. [260].
## 5.4.1. Concluding remarks and future perspectives
In this review, the application of MOF-based nanocatalysts in the field of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ fixation and reduction is deliberated. An overview of six methods, namely, photocatalytic reduction, electrocatalytic reduction, photoelectrocatalytic reduction, catalytic hydrogenation, cycloaddition reaction with epoxides, and the reaction of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ with terminal alkynes, is provided for the chemical fixation and conversion of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ into economically valuable products like methanol, ethanol, methane, olefins, and carbon monoxide. In the background of global warming and the greenhouse effect, this comprehensive review covers most of the recent advancements in the field of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction. Due to the various advantages such as high surface area, good stability, permanent porosity, and presence of multiple active centers, MOF-based nanocatalysts have opened up newer pathways for more efficient ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction. It offers an edge over traditional catalysts due to its better recyclability and ease of catalyst removal, hence there is substantial scope for further explorations. Photoreduction is one of the most promising methods for efficient $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction and a myriad of assorted MOF-based photocatalysts have been reported in the last few years (Table 2). The integration of homogeneous catalysts or semiconductors with MOF, strategies for increasing the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ uptake capacity, light absorption ability, and electron transfer efficiency, are some of the major focus areas for future studies. The economic viability will have to be evaluated in detail as some of the presently deployed photosensitizers are rather expensive and a search for more economic alternatives is warranted in future research besides eliminating the need for a sacrificial agent, which is not ecofriendly. In most cases, MOF-based photocatalysts provide increased stability in addition to other desired characteristics. One such example can be seen in the case of $\mathsf{I}(\mathsf{Z r}_{6}\mathsf{O}_{4}(\mathsf{O H})_{4}(\mathrm{L})$ 6DMF) which is a porous MOF structure that shows excellent chemical stability. [92] Explicitly, current research suggests that an increase in stability is exhibited by most of the MOF-based nanocomposites compared to parent compounds, possibly due to the porous character of the derivatives. This makes these composites more useful in research, especially as heterogeneous catalysts. Electrocatalytic $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction is another appealing method for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction as it provides many advantages over conventional methods such as easier separation of products, optimum reaction rate, and catalyst selectivity. The annual advances in this field have been discussed in detail and promising electrocatalysts with high surface area, schematic charge transport, and ample active sites have been identified. Even though limitations like low current density and poor product selectivity have been addressed, further explorations are needed to ensure the economic viability of electrocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction. Photoelectrocatalysis is a budding technology that outshines both individual photocatalysis and electrocatalysis. Very few MOF-based photoelectrocatalysts have been developed so far, and thus, there is a vast scope of research in the coming years for this technology. Catalytic hydrogenation is another promising field of research due to the variety of valuable products that can be produced. The focus here must be on MOF nanocomposites which exhibit a catalytic effect at room temperature and atmospheric pressure. Although these MOF-based composites have various advantages over other catalysts, other aspects like poison tests need to be undertaken in the future. The cycloaddition of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ with epoxides generally occurs at elevated temperatures and pressure. Since recent studies have revealed that the utilization of multi-variate sites of catalysts and the introduction of light during the reaction are very effective, accordingly, future studies ought to focus on means to develop such efficient and economical MOF composites. It is important to note that at present, MOF-based catalysts are not the best or most convenient catalysts in the cycloaddition of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to epoxides where high catalytic activity can be obtained at room temperature by systems that do not involve reticular chemistry. Carboxylation of terminal alkynes with ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ is a promising approach to transforming ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ into valuable products. Moreover, it is a considerably greener method for the preparation of alkynyl carboxylic acids, which serve as important synthetic intermediates and building blocks in the chemical and pharmaceutical industries. With homogeneous catalysts, the recovery of the catalyst and reusability is very difficult. The employment of heterogeneous catalysts based on MOFs helps circumvent these impediments. In spite of these innovations, there is enough room for further progress for enhanced practical application, particularly in the following areas: (1) improvement in the chemical, mechanical and thermal stability of MOF-based catalysts, (2) more effectual, quick and scalable synthetic strategies for the preparation of MOF composites for large-scale production, (3) well-defined and deeper understanding of the synergetic effect between the functional material and MOFs to fabricate more efficient catalysts, (4) exploration of more transformation reactions of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ using MOF-based catalysts at room temperature, atmospheric pressure and low concentrations of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , (5) accomplishing low-cost, large-scale production of MOFs, (6) synthesis of MOFs is usually an energy-consuming process as several steps of organic synthesis are required for the synthesis of certain organic linkers and some metal precursors are remarkably costly, (7) high conductivity is preferred for rapid charge transfer in both photocatalysis and electrocatalysis. Consequently, the poor conductivity of MOF catalysts should be improved, (8) the majority of the present studies over MOF-based catalysts are based on model reactions. Intensive investigations on the industrially important and challenging reactions over MOF-based materials are ought to be explored, (9) there are only a few pristine MOFs possessing both high stability and abundant active sites, which contradicts their practical application in catalysis, (10) Despite the praiseworthy progress in the fabrication of MOF-based nanomaterials, the intrinsic mechanism of their transformation and possible changes during catalytic reactions are still ambiguous and unclear, highly limiting their rational design and application.
No data was used for the research described in the article.
